@node Session identity
@section Session identity

@findex agent-session
@findex agent-session-buffer-name
Every live session buffer carries a buffer-local @code{agent-session} struct@comma{} captured once when the session starts. Its slots are @code{backend} (the backend symbol)@comma{} @code{account} (the account name@comma{} or nil for the default)@comma{} @code{directory} (the abbreviated project directory)@comma{} @code{instance} (the instance name@comma{} or nil)@comma{} and @code{id} (the CLI session id@comma{} or nil until known). The function @code{agent-session} returns the struct for a buffer@comma{} and @code{agent-session-buffer-name} derives the canonical buffer name from the identity. All orchestration code reads identity from this struct; nothing parses buffer names.

@findex agent-session-buffers
@vindex agent-session-id-functions
The @code{id} slot holds the backend's native session id and is maintained for the whole session lifetime: a non-fork resume seeds it in @code{agent-start-session}@comma{} the Claude backend records the @code{session_id} reported by every status poll (so a branch switch@comma{} which changes the id@comma{} updates it)@comma{} and the Codex backend records the id @code{codex-session-identity} reports on every CLI hook event. A fresh session therefore acquires its id within one status poll interval (Claude) or at its first hook event or prompt submission@comma{} whichever comes first (Codex); a fresh app-server Codex session knows its thread id before any CLI hook event fires@comma{} so the submission hook is the earliest per-buffer signal. The slot only records the id: it does not select a conversation. To resume one@comma{} pass @code{:resume-id} to @code{agent-start-session}; @code{(agent-session-create :id x)} on its own would start a fresh session@comma{} so @code{agent-start-session} refuses a session that carries an id without @code{:resume-id} rather than silently losing the conversation the caller meant to continue. A handoff starts a fresh conversation@comma{} so its new session drops the source's id. Whenever the recorded id changes@comma{} the abnormal hook @code{agent-session-id-functions} runs with the session buffer@comma{} so optional integrations — such as the Agent Log bridge — can observe identity without reading private variables. The public surface for such integrations is @code{agent-session}@comma{} the @code{agent-session-id} accessor@comma{} @code{agent-session-buffers} (all live session buffers across backends)@comma{} and @code{agent-session-display-state}.

@node Session state machine
@section Session state machine

@findex agent-session-event
Each session buffer tracks its readiness in the buffer-local variable @code{agent--session-state}@comma{} which is one of @code{unknown}@comma{} @code{busy}@comma{} @code{awaiting-input}@comma{} or @code{closing}. Buffers start as @code{unknown} and stay there until a session event arrives@comma{} so a state that was never observed is never presented as one that was; the session switcher renders such sessions with @code{agent-unknown} and @code{agent-jump-to-waiting} skips them. All transitions go through @code{agent-session-event}@comma{} which accepts the events @code{stop}@comma{} @code{idle-prompt}@comma{} @code{blocked}@comma{} @code{submit}@comma{} @code{activity}@comma{} and @code{exit-request}. An @code{activity} event marks the session busy on evidence that it is working. It exists because turns the user did not type in Emacs — one driven by remote control@comma{} a scheduled task@comma{} or a resumed background job — would otherwise be unobservable@comma{} leaving the session displayed as waiting while it works. Claude Code has no busy/idle state of its own@comma{} so the backend assembles one from its hooks. The bundled @samp{notify-emacs-state.sh} wrapper forwards the @samp{UserPromptSubmit}@comma{} @samp{PreToolUse}@comma{} @samp{PostToolUse}@comma{} @samp{PostToolUseFailure}@comma{} and @samp{SubagentStart} hooks as @code{activity}@comma{} whoever started the turn@comma{} and @samp{Stop} and @samp{StopFailure} — a turn ended by an API error@comma{} which fires no @samp{Stop} hook — as @code{stop}. @code{agent-claude-mode} adds its own functions to @code{claude-code-event-hook} ahead of other packages' functions@comma{} at @code{agent-claude--event-hook-depth}. Claude Code runs that hook only until a function returns non-nil@comma{} so a function from another package that returned a value would otherwise hide every later event from the session state; in November 2026 one that returned a timer on each stop left sessions marked busy after their turns ended. The tool hooks also return a session to busy once the user answers a permission dialog. Because these hooks fire on every tool call of every session and subagent@comma{} most of them report a session Emacs already marks busy. To keep that traffic off the Emacs server@comma{} @code{agent-claude--record-state} mirrors each Claude session's state to a file in @code{agent-claude-status-directory}@comma{} keyed by the session's per-process UUID like its statusline file@comma{} and the wrapper skips an @code{activity} event while that file reads @samp{busy}. Emacs passes each Claude process its UUID in @samp{AGENT_SESSION_UUID} when it starts it@comma{} and hands the same UUID to the session buffer by the buffer's name. Sessions started at the same time therefore cannot take each other's UUID@. A session left without one would have no status line@comma{} no known session id and no transcript on the phone page. A missing or unreadable file sends the event as before@comma{} and a failed write deletes the file. Other backends can observe transitions through @code{agent-session-state-change-functions}. Each forwarded event carries its send time@comma{} and @code{agent-claude--stale-hook-event-p} discards one sent before the session last began waiting@comma{} because background delivery can land a turn's final tool event after its stop event. The @samp{Stop} hook delivers in the background too@comma{} so that a stalled Emacs server never holds up the end of a turn@comma{} and each forwarded call waits at most ten seconds for the server; a stall can then deliver one turn's stop after the next turn has begun. @code{agent-claude--stale-stop-p} therefore discards a @code{stop} sent before the event that began the session's current turn: the first hook event of the turn@comma{} or@comma{} for a turn without one@comma{} such as a prompt sent from Emacs@comma{} the moment Emacs marked the session busy. A hook that fires inside a subagent carries the subagent's @samp{agent_id}@comma{} which the wrapper forwards too. A background subagent keeps calling tools after the main turn ends and while the main thread waits at a dialog@comma{} so @code{agent-claude--subagent-hook-event-p} ignores a subagent's event while the session awaits input@comma{} unless the session is blocked on a dialog that same subagent raised: the Notification payload names the subagent behind a permission or elicitation dialog@comma{} and @code{agent-claude--blocking-agent} records it@comma{} or @samp{main} for the main thread@comma{} until the session's state next changes. A session whose background subagent is working therefore stays waiting@comma{} shown with @code{agent-waiting-with-background} when the backend reports the task. A turn Claude starts without a prompt and runs without tools fires none of these hooks@comma{} so the status-line poll remains as a fallback: Claude's status line reports a fresh @code{prompt_id} for every turn whatever its origin@comma{} and @code{agent-claude--detect-turn-start} emits @code{activity} when the identifier changes@comma{} within one poll interval@comma{} @code{agent-claude-status-interval}. A turn shorter than that interval both begins and ends between polls@comma{} and its @code{stop} event has already landed by the time the change is seen@comma{} so that case is detected and skipped rather than stranding the session as busy. Interrupting a turn fires no hook at all@comma{} so an escape sent with @code{claude-code-send-escape} to a busy session schedules @code{agent-claude--check-interrupt}: after @code{agent-claude-interrupt-check-delay} seconds@comma{} if the session is still busy with no state change since the escape and its terminal shows the idle input prompt@comma{} the session is marked @code{blocked}. An interrupt from outside Emacs@comma{} such as through remote control@comma{} is not detected until the next turn ends. A @code{blocked} event marks the session as waiting on the user without firing a ready alert@comma{} and is used for permission and elicitation dialogs: Claude reaches those from inside a turn with a tool call still in flight@comma{} so nothing else reports them as waiting even though they proceed only once the user answers. Backends translate raw CLI notifications into these events and never set session state directly. A @code{stop} or @code{idle-prompt} event first offers the transition to the before-exit chain; when the chain consumes it@comma{} ready alerts and display refreshes are suppressed. Ready alerts fire only for @code{idle-prompt} events@comma{} and a @code{submit} event delivered while the session is already busy is ignored@comma{} because backend submission hooks can fire more than once per submission.

@node Session teardown and leak policy
@section Session teardown and leak policy

Session resource cleanup has a single owner: @code{agent--session-teardown} runs a session buffer's @code{agent--teardown-functions} exactly once@comma{} whether the buffer is killed first or the CLI process exits first. Backends and features register their per-session resources (status poll timers@comma{} monet servers@comma{} before-exit chains) as teardown functions instead of installing their own kill-buffer hooks. A session key has buffer lifetime instead: assigning one atomically installs its buffer-kill cleanup@comma{} while session teardown can release it early after process exit. Claude buffers also adopt any already-running Monet server when @code{agent-claude-mode} is enabled or reloaded. The monet websocket server needs one extra guard: when the Claude client disconnects@comma{} @code{monet--on-close-server} drops the session from monet's own table before teardown can look it up@comma{} so an @samp{:after} advice closes the listening server at that moment rather than letting it escape to the GC safety net. Resources that escape teardown are not silently reaped: safety nets report each one through a @samp{Warnings} buffer entry tagged @samp{agent}@comma{} naming the leaked resource. A leak warning therefore indicates a lifecycle bug worth reporting@comma{} not routine noise. The Monet GC sweep identifies servers by ownership@comma{} not by process name: Agent registers every server process it sees monet create or adopt@comma{} and the sweep only ever reaps registered servers that have lost their monet session. Websocket servers belonging to other packages (atomic-chrome@comma{} org-roam-ui) share the generic @samp{websocket server on port N} process name but are invisible to the sweep and can never be reported or killed as monet leaks.

@node Account model
@section Account model

@findex agent-account-select
@findex agent-account-init
Multi-account state lives in @samp{agent-account.el} and is keyed by backend. Each backend declares an accounts alist mapping account names to per-account configuration homes (@code{agent-claude-accounts}@comma{} @code{agent-codex-accounts})@comma{} a persisted selection file that survives Emacs restarts (@code{agent-claude-account-file}@comma{} @code{agent-codex-account-file})@comma{} and the environment variable that points the CLI at the selected home: @samp{CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR} for Claude Code and @samp{CODEX_HOME} for Codex. Account homes keep credentials separate@comma{} while shared state (settings@comma{} hooks@comma{} skills@comma{} sessions@comma{} history) is symlinked from the backend's canonical home so it remains available after switching accounts; conflicting account-local files are backed up to timestamped @samp{*.agent-backup-*} siblings first. Codex hook trust is keyed by the lexical @samp{hooks.json} path rather than the symlink target@comma{} so account sync copies the canonical path's persisted trust state to the account path. It copies existing hashes rather than computing new ones; a changed canonical hook therefore remains untrusted. Any sync failure aborts session startup instead of letting a session run with missing or stale policy. @code{agent-account-select} persists a choice and syncs the account home@comma{} and @code{agent-account-init} prepares or repairs a home. The account infix in @code{agent-menu} always asks which backend to change before it asks for an account; it never silently infers the backend from the buffer where the menu happened to be opened. When the merge engine reads a home's @samp{.claude.json}@comma{} a parse failure is retried over increasing delays before warning@comma{} since a running CLI rewrites the file frequently and a read can land mid-write; a failure that persists is warned about once@comma{} with the invalid bytes preserved in a snapshot file so a torn read can be told apart from real corruption. Writes go to a temporary file that is renamed into place@comma{} so concurrent readers never observe a truncated @samp{.claude.json}. The account a session was started under is recorded in its @code{agent-session} struct@comma{} so restart and handoff preserve it even after the global selection changes.

@findex agent-account-property
@findex agent-account-route
An accounts entry is either @samp{(NAME . HOME)} or @samp{(NAME :home HOME :pool POOL ...)}. The plist form carries arbitrary further properties@comma{} read with @code{agent-account-property}@comma{} for integrations that need per-account settings beyond the home. Accounts sharing a @code{:pool} are interchangeable: they hold the same configuration and differ only in whose quota they spend. The selection prompt lists pools ahead of accounts@comma{} and selecting a pool persists the pool name@comma{} so @code{agent-account-current} may answer with a pool; @code{agent-account-resolve} always answers with a concrete account@comma{} routing a pool selection through @code{agent-account-route}. Selecting a pool syncs every member's home@comma{} so routing never has to touch the filesystem. A member chosen by name pins that account as before. A pool may carry the same name as one of its members — convenient when an existing selection file names the first of what became several equivalent accounts — in which case the name is listed once and selects the pool@comma{} so that member can no longer be pinned by name.

@vindex agent-account-route-hysteresis
@findex agent-account-usage-score
Routing skips members whose credentials are missing@comma{} then sets aside members whose latest fresh usage reading (@ref{Account usage, , Account usage}) flags them as limited or shows either window at 100%. The rest are ranked by @code{agent-account-usage-score}: the larger of the weekly and session percentages@comma{} with the weekly share breaking ties@comma{} so lower is better; a member with no fresh reading ranks after every member with one. The member the pool was last routed to is kept unless a sibling beats it by @code{agent-account-route-hysteresis} points (default @samp{10})@comma{} so near-equal members do not alternate between consecutive sessions — except that a current member with unknown usage never holds off a sibling with a known score. When every member is limited@comma{} the one whose window resets soonest is returned@comma{} and the echo area says so@comma{} so the session start can still report the limit. A route that changes hands is announced with the chosen member's usage; a resolution that keeps the previous member stays silent@comma{} since routing also runs for background processes and would otherwise flood the echo area. Every routing decision is followed by a refresh of the pool's readings. A restart of a session whose account belongs to the selected pool reruns routing without prompting@comma{} so an exhausted member can be replaced by one with capacity. Both backends share session history between account homes@comma{} so the conversation is preserved.

@vindex agent-account-directory-rules
@findex agent-account-directory-selection
The persisted selection is global@comma{} so on its own it would start a session for one organization's project under whichever account was last selected for another. @code{agent-account-directory-rules} ties directories to accounts: each rule is @samp{(DIRECTORY . SELECTION)}@comma{} where SELECTION is an account or pool name for every backend or an alist of @samp{(BACKEND . NAME)} entries@comma{} and it governs DIRECTORY and every directory below it@comma{} with symbolic links resolved on both sides. When no rule covers a session's directory@comma{} the rules are matched against its common Git directory@comma{} so a linked worktree checked out elsewhere follows the rule of its repository; a repository whose Git directory lives apart from its working tree (@samp{git init --separate-git-dir}) needs a rule for that Git directory for its worktrees to be covered. The first matching rule wins@comma{} and a backend missing from a rule's alist is not governed by it. @code{agent-account-resolve} consults the rule for the session's directory after the account of a session being started and before the persisted selection@comma{} so a rule naming a pool still routes among its members. To make that possible@comma{} @code{agent-start-session} fills a session's missing directory from the backend's ambient directory (the current buffer's project) before it resolves the account@comma{} and the commands that start a new session do the same; the backends' environment hooks and non-interactive Claude runs resolve against the directory they run in@comma{} and @code{agent-restart} compares the session's account with the one its directory's rule names rather than the global selection. A rule naming an account or pool the backend does not declare signals an error instead of falling back to the persisted selection. An account the caller passes explicitly@comma{} such as the one recorded on a session being restarted@comma{} handed off or restored@comma{} still wins@comma{} so sessions that are already running keep their account. For example:

@lisp
(setopt agent-account-directory-rules
        '(("~/repos/epoch/" . ((claude-code . "epoch") (codex . "epoch-pool")))
          ("~/My Drive/Forethought/" . "forethought")))
@end lisp

@vindex agent-account-origin-rules
A thing routed with @code{agent-act-on-thing-at-point} has no directory yet@comma{} so the directory rules cannot pick the account whose project sources it is offered. @code{agent-account-origin-rules} ties where the thing comes from to an account instead: each rule is @samp{(REGEXP . SELECTION)}@comma{} with SELECTION shaped as in the directory rules@comma{} and the first rule whose REGEXP matches the thing's origin names the account. A Slack message's origin is @samp{slack:} followed by the name its team was registered under@comma{} and an email's is @samp{mu4e:} followed by its maildir; other things have none@comma{} and a thing whose origin no rule matches uses the persisted selection. For example:

@lisp
(setopt agent-account-origin-rules
        '(("\\`slack:Forethought\\'" . "forethought")
          ("\\`mu4e:/forethought/" . "forethought")))
@end lisp

@findex agent-account-login
Credentials are part of the account model too. A backend may declare the account-local credentials file inside each home (@samp{auth.json} for Codex) and the CLI arguments of its login flow. @code{agent-account-login} — bound to @samp{a l} in @code{agent-menu} — runs that login flow with the selected account's environment@comma{} so the credentials always land in the right account home; running the bare CLI login in a terminal would write to the backend's default home instead. The command shows the login output@comma{} including the authentication URL@comma{} in a dedicated buffer and reports success or failure when the flow finishes. Session starts guard on the same declaration: when an account's credentials file is missing — after the in-session logout command@comma{} or when tokens were removed out of band — @code{agent-start-session} refuses to spawn a session that could only fail to authenticate and points at @code{agent-account-login} instead. Backends that do not declare a credentials file (Claude Code stores credentials in the macOS Keychain) are assumed logged in and never blocked.

@node Account usage
@section Account usage

@vindex agent-usage-interval
@vindex agent-usage-max-interval
@vindex agent-usage-request-timeout
@vindex agent-usage-cache-file
@vindex agent-usage-stale-after
@findex agent-usage-get
@findex agent-usage-fetch
@findex agent-usage-failure
@findex agent-usage-http-get
Per-account usage lives in @samp{agent-usage.el} and is keyed by backend and account. A backend that can report usage declares a @code{:usage-fetch} slot: a function called with an account name and a callback@comma{} which it calls with a normalized plist@comma{} or with a failure from @code{agent-usage-failure} that gives the reason and@comma{} when the server sent one@comma{} its @samp{Retry-After} delay. The plist carries @code{:session-pct} and @code{:weekly-pct} (percent of the short and long windows used)@comma{} @code{:session-reset} and @code{:weekly-reset} (reset times as float seconds)@comma{} @code{:limited} (non-nil when the backend refuses further requests)@comma{} and @code{:fetched-at}. Claude reads the undocumented @samp{api/oauth/usage} endpoint with the Keychain OAuth token; Codex reads the ChatGPT @samp{wham/usage} endpoint with the access token and account id stored in the account home's @samp{auth.json}@comma{} classifying a window of at least a day as weekly and a shorter one as the session window@comma{} so a plan with a single weekly window reports only that. @code{agent-usage-get} answers the latest reading and @code{agent-usage-fetch} refreshes one account.

One poller serves every backend. It starts with the first session of any backend and stops when the last one is torn down@comma{} polling every account that has a live session plus every member of every pool@comma{} so routing has data for idle members too. @code{agent-usage-interval} defaults to @samp{300} seconds. A failed fetch keeps the account's last reading and records the reason on it as @code{:error}. A request that got no HTTP answer at all@comma{} such as one cut off while the machine slept@comma{} is a network failure (@code{agent-usage-network-failure}): it says nothing about the account@comma{} so it is retried at the next poll and announced nowhere but the usage buffer. Any other failure also records @code{:retry-at}@comma{} the time before which the poller and pool refreshes leave the account alone. The wait is the server's @samp{Retry-After} when it sent one@comma{} however long; otherwise the account's own wait starts at @code{agent-usage-interval} and doubles with each consecutive failure up to @code{agent-usage-max-interval}@comma{} which defaults to @samp{900}. Backoff is per account@comma{} so one failing account neither slows the others nor is reset by their successes. @samp{g} in the usage buffer skips the backoff but still honours a server's @samp{Retry-After}. The echo area reports such a failure when its reason changes and again when the account recovers. A Claude token the endpoint rejects is reported as needing Claude Code to start as that account@comma{} which renews it. A Codex access token that has expired is reported as needing Codex to run as that account@comma{} since the Codex CLI refreshes an account's token only when it runs with that home. Readings are written to @code{agent-usage-cache-file} after every fetch and read back on first use@comma{} so a fresh Emacs can route before its first poll completes; a reading older than @code{agent-usage-stale-after} seconds no longer counts for routing. The Claude status helpers @code{agent-claude-status-session-usage} and friends read this store and keep answering ISO timestamps for the reset times. Both backends send their usage requests through @code{agent-usage-http-get}@comma{} which runs @samp{curl} as a subprocess@comma{} so a slow DNS lookup or a connection that never answers cannot block Emacs@comma{} as @code{url-retrieve} can while it resolves the host and negotiates TLS@. A request unanswered after @code{agent-usage-request-timeout} seconds@comma{} @samp{30} by default@comma{} or one that cannot start@comma{} is a network failure. The request headers@comma{} which carry the account's token@comma{} reach @samp{curl} on its standard input rather than its command line.

@findex agent-usage-show
@code{agent-usage-show} — bound to @samp{a u} in @code{agent-menu} — lists every configured account@comma{} live-session account@comma{} and pool member in a @samp{*agent-usage*} buffer with its pool@comma{} session and weekly percentages@comma{} weekly reset time@comma{} limit flag@comma{} the age of the reading@comma{} and the last fetch error with its retry time (a network error@comma{} dimmed@comma{} with the time it happened)@comma{} then fetches fresh readings and re-renders as each arrives; @samp{g} refreshes again. Configured accounts appear even without a live session; backends without configured or active accounts show their default account. Background polling remains limited to live-session accounts and pool members. The limit flag and the percentages are what routing acts on@comma{} so the buffer shows at a glance why a pool went to the member it did. A reading older than @code{agent-usage-stale-after}@comma{} which routing ignores@comma{} is dimmed and its age highlighted@comma{} and a window whose reset time has passed shows no percentage or reset@comma{} since the reading no longer describes the current window.

@node Backend minor modes
@section Backend minor modes

@findex agent-claude-mode
@findex agent-codex-mode
The global minor modes @code{agent-claude-mode} and @code{agent-codex-mode} own every hook@comma{} advice@comma{} timer@comma{} and notification handler their backend installs. Loading @samp{agent-claude.el} or @samp{agent-codex.el} installs nothing; the modes must be enabled explicitly. Disabling a mode removes its hooks and advice symmetrically and restores the backend's previous notification function@comma{} leaving running sessions untouched. A typical setup:

@lisp
(use-package agent
  :config
  (require 'agent-claude)
  (require 'agent-codex)
  (require 'agent-chief)
  (agent-claude-mode 1)
  (agent-codex-mode 1))
@end lisp

