The user option @code{agent-protect-buffers} is a boolean that defaults to @code{t}. When enabled@comma{} killing a live AI session buffer prompts for confirmation through @code{agent-protect-buffer}. Buffers whose processes have already exited are allowed to die without the prompt.

The user option @code{agent-warn-kill-with-branches} is a boolean that defaults to @code{t}. When enabled@comma{} killing a session that other sessions were branched from asks a second confirmation naming how many branches it has@comma{} after the ordinary kill-protection prompt. The check scans only the sessions that could descend from the one being killed@comma{} and any failure to scan allows the kill@comma{} so a session never becomes unkillable because its transcripts moved. It replaces @code{agent-claude-warn-kill-with-branches}@comma{} which is kept as an obsolete alias; that option was declared but never read by any code@comma{} so setting it had no effect until the check described here existed.

The user option @code{agent-branch-worktree-directory} names the base directory for the git worktrees @code{agent-create-branch} creates with a prefix argument (@ref{Branch navigation, , Branch navigation}). It defaults to a location under the cache directory@comma{} away from cloud sync@comma{} and replaces @code{agent-claude-fork-worktree-directory}@comma{} which is kept as an obsolete alias. The default path still ends in the literal segment @samp{claude-worktrees}@comma{} unchanged from the Claude-only original so that worktrees created before the rename are still found where they were left.

The user option @code{agent-alert-on-ready} is a boolean that defaults to @code{nil}. When enabled@comma{} backend notification handlers can call @code{agent-notify} to emit visual and/or audible alerts when a session is waiting for input. Codex's own terminal-bell notification path is routed through the same alert setting. When a visual alert shows the notification@comma{} its text goes to the @samp{*Messages*} buffer only@comma{} not the echo area@comma{} and so does the copy that styles such as @samp{osx-notifier} echo; without a visual alert the text is echoed once.  Approval and input requests@comma{} which hold the session until the user answers@comma{} are always echoed as well@comma{} since a desktop notification can be hidden@comma{} for instance by Do Not Disturb. @code{agent-alert-style} selects @code{visual}@comma{} @code{sound}@comma{} or @code{both}; it defaults to @code{both}. @code{agent-alert-sound} defaults to @code{nil}; set it to a sound file to enable sound alerts. @code{agent-alert-sound-player} optionally names an external sound player when Emacs sound support is unavailable.

The user option @code{agent-backtrace-file} controls where @code{agent-save-backtrace} and @code{agent-debug-backtrace} write Emacs backtraces. It defaults to a file in @code{temporary-file-directory}.

The unified orchestration commands share one set of options across backends. @code{agent-handoff-files} maps backend symbols to the handoff files read by @code{agent-handoff}. @code{agent-debug-backtrace-model} and @code{agent-debug-backtrace-backend} configure the @samp{gptel} request used by @code{agent-debug-backtrace} to identify likely culprit packages from an Emacs backtrace.

Slack-message routing has no options of its own any more. The model and @samp{gptel} backend that pick a project from the message are the shared @code{agent-project-ranking-model} and @code{agent-project-ranking-backend} (@ref{Project sources, , Project sources})@comma{} and @code{agent-act-on-slack-message-model} and @code{agent-act-on-slack-message-backend} are obsolete aliases of them. The project candidates come from @code{agent-project-sources}@comma{} so a registry that used to be named by @code{agent-epoch-project-registry-file} now belongs in that alist: that option is obsolete and nothing reads it@comma{} which means a configuration setting it alone routes against the default source list instead of the registry. Loading @samp{agent-project} warns when that option still holds a value@comma{} because marking a variable obsolete warns only code that mentions it and says nothing to a configuration that merely sets it@comma{} which is the whole of what such a configuration does. @code{agent-epoch-projects-root} is an obsolete alias of @code{agent-project-registry-root} and still resolves the registry's relative paths. The function @code{agent-epoch-project-candidates}@comma{} which read that registry into project plists@comma{} is replaced by @code{agent-project-candidates}@comma{} which reads every source an account has and answers in a different shape. The old name is marked obsolete but no longer defined@comma{} so a configuration still calling it is warned at byte-compile time which name replaced it@comma{} and fails if it runs. All three renamings are declared in @samp{agent-project.el} ahead of the options they rename@comma{} because a setting made before the package loads — which is what @code{use-package}'s @code{:custom} does — reaches the new name only while that name is still unbound.

The user option @code{agent-prompt-capture-directory} controls where @code{agent-capture-prompt} stores per-session Org files. Each file is keyed by backend@comma{} account@comma{} project directory@comma{} and session instance@comma{} so prompts are linked to the session they target and remain available after Emacs restarts. @code{agent-prompt-capture-auto-save-delay} controls the idle delay before capture buffers are saved automatically.

The user option @code{agent-sync-theme} is a boolean that defaults to @code{nil}. When enabled@comma{} theme changes in Emacs are persisted through each registered backend's theme-sync handler. This updates configuration files for new or restarted CLI sessions without sending theme commands into active terminal conversations.

The user option @code{agent-sigwinch-delay} is a number of seconds and defaults to @samp{0.5}. It controls how long @code{agent-fix-rendering} waits before sending @samp{SIGWINCH} to a terminal process. Increase it if an AI CLI reads the terminal size before the @samp{eat} window has settled.

The user option @code{agent-session-annotation-max-width} caps the width of session annotations in the session switcher (@ref{Shared commands, , Shared commands}). It defaults to @code{nil}@comma{} which applies no cap beyond the fit to the frame. An integer narrows annotations to at most that many columns; it never widens them past what the frame holds@comma{} since the frame fit applies either way. Text longer than the resulting width is truncated with an ellipsis. A cap of @samp{0} leaves no room at all@comma{} and every session then renders as the bare label it would have without an annotation@comma{} rather than a padded label followed by nothing.

Snippet helpers use @samp{yasnippet} and @samp{consult-yasnippet} only when those optional packages are loaded.

@node Project sources
@section Project sources

@vindex agent-project-sources
@vindex agent-project-registry-root
@vindex agent-project-ranking-model
@vindex agent-project-ranking-backend
@findex agent-project-candidates
@findex agent-project-read
The @samp{agent-project} module enumerates the projects a session can be started in when the thing the session acts on carries no directory of its own. The user option @code{agent-project-sources} is an alist of (REGEXP . SOURCES). The regexp is searched for anywhere in the account name of the backend that will run the session — anchor it with @samp{\`} and @samp{\'} to match a whole name@comma{} since a bare @samp{"epoch"} also matches an account called @samp{epoch-personal} — and the first matching entry wins@comma{} so an empty regexp is a catch-all that also covers a backend with no account selected. It defaults to one catch-all entry listing @code{project-known} followed by @samp{~/repos/*}@comma{} @samp{~/code/*}@comma{} @samp{~/src/*}@comma{} @samp{~/projects/*} and @samp{~/Developer/*}; a directory that does not exist contributes nothing.

Each source is the symbol @code{project-known}@comma{} a function symbol@comma{} a registry file@comma{} whose name ends in @samp{.json}@comma{} a @samp{(FILE . ROOT)} pair naming a registry file together with its root@comma{} or a path pattern. @code{project-known} stands for the local projects Emacs remembers@comma{} as listed by @code{project-known-project-roots}; remote roots and roots that no longer exist are passed over. Any other symbol names a function called with no arguments that returns project directories@comma{} which are offered whether or not they are repositories; @samp{forethought-project-dirs} is one.

A path pattern may contain wildcards. A wildcard is @samp{*}@comma{} @samp{?}@comma{} or a @samp{[...]} character class@comma{} and a pattern containing one is expanded a single level per wildcard component: @samp{~/repos/*} reaches the children of @samp{~/repos/}@comma{} and @samp{~/repos/.worktrees/*/*} its grandchildren two levels down. Wildcard expansion keeps only git repositories and worktrees; a worktree records its git directory in a file rather than a directory@comma{} and both count. A pattern without a wildcard names exactly one directory@comma{} which is kept whether or not it is a repository@comma{} and dropped when it does not exist or is not a directory. A pattern that cannot be expanded at all@comma{} such as one holding an unbalanced @samp{[}@comma{} is reported in the echo area and skipped@comma{} so one malformed source never suppresses the candidates of the others.

A registry is a JSON file holding a @samp{projects} array@comma{} in the format the obsolete @code{agent-epoch-project-registry-file} used to read (@ref{Shared session behavior, , Shared session behavior}). Every entry becomes one candidate@comma{} labelled by its @samp{id} and @samp{title}@comma{} or by its @samp{id} alone when it records no title. Unlike a path pattern it carries a description: the entry's @samp{summary}@comma{} or its @samp{aliases} when it records no summary@comma{} followed by its @samp{browser_keywords} and its Slack channels; an entry recording none of the four has no description at all rather than an empty one. The keywords are worth their length: they are the terms the project is recognized by elsewhere@comma{} repository slugs and domain names among them@comma{} which is what a routed Slack message or email is likeliest to name@comma{} whereas aliases are often the title restated. An entry that records a JSON @samp{null} where a string belongs counts as recording nothing@comma{} so a null summary falls back to the aliases just as an omitted one does. Later stages use that text to judge which project a piece of text is about. An entry's directory is its first @samp{project_doc_paths} folder when it records one@comma{} because that folder holds the project's instruction files@comma{} its first @samp{repo_paths} directory when it records only that@comma{} and the registry's root itself when it records neither. Relative paths are expanded against the root of the registry that records them@comma{} and each registry finds its root in this order: the @samp{ROOT} of a @samp{(FILE . ROOT)} source; for a registry at @samp{.../projects/shared/project-registry.json}@comma{} that @samp{projects} directory two levels above the file; and finally the user option @code{agent-project-registry-root}@comma{} which defaults to @code{nil}. Each registry therefore resolves against its own directory@comma{} so an account listing a shared registry and a personal one gets each project where its own registry places it. A registry with no root by any of these routes can still be read while every path it records is absolute; a relative path in it raises an error. A source naming a registry that does not exist raises an error rather than being skipped@comma{} since it points at a file the user meant to have.

The function @code{agent-project-candidates} returns the candidates for an account as plists with @code{:label}@comma{} @code{:directory}@comma{} @code{:repository} and @code{:description}. The repository is the candidate's local clone: the directory itself for a path source that is a git repository or worktree@comma{} the first @samp{repo_paths} directory for a registry entry@comma{} and nil for a project holding no clone of its own. It is what @code{agent-project-repository-directory} answers from@comma{} which returns the clone of a named repository for an account@comma{} or nil when no source holds one that exists. Forge routing asks it for a repository whose worktree Forge does not know or no longer knows@comma{} which is what a repository that moved looks like (@ref{Shared commands, , Shared commands}). Candidates are deduplicated by true path@comma{} so listing the same directory twice under different names@comma{} or through a symbolic link@comma{} yields one entry. A label shared by two candidates is replaced with the abbreviated directory@comma{} which is what then appears in completion@comma{} so no two projects offer the same string.

The function @code{agent-project-read} asks for one of those candidates. It takes the account@comma{} the text of the thing being routed@comma{} the completion prompt@comma{} a callback and an optional suggested directory@comma{} and calls the callback with the directory of the project chosen. A suggestion is offered first@comma{} which makes it what a bare @samp{RET} answers@comma{} and it joins the candidates when no source holds it@comma{} so a caller that worked its directory out for itself leads the choice instead of bypassing it. Nothing is ranked when a suggestion is given@comma{} since a caller that already knows the project has no use for a model's opinion of it. It answers through the callback even when nothing is ranked@comma{} so every caller reads the answer the same way: the directory arrives in the callback@comma{} never as a return value. An account whose sources contribute no candidate at all raises an error rather than offering an empty prompt.

The candidates are ordered by a model before completion whenever there is something to order: the routed text must hold more than whitespace@comma{} and there must be more than one candidate. Descriptions are not required. A candidate that came from a path pattern alone@comma{} or from a registry entry whose summary@comma{} aliases@comma{} keywords and Slack channels are all empty@comma{} is described to the model by its label@comma{} which is the project's name@comma{} and a name such as @samp{white-glove} is enough to match a text against. An earlier rule ranked only when at least one candidate carried a description@comma{} which meant an account whose sources were all path patterns was never ranked at all@comma{} whatever was routed. When the request is made@comma{} @code{agent-project-ranking-model} and @code{agent-project-ranking-backend} name the model and the @samp{gptel} backend it runs on; they default to @code{gemini-flash-lite-latest} on the @samp{"Gemini"} backend@comma{} and ranking requires @samp{gptel}@comma{} which is otherwise an optional dependency. The request is sent from @code{temporary-file-directory} rather than the current buffer's directory: a Slack callback runs in whatever buffer happens to be current@comma{} and when that buffer's directory has been deleted@comma{} starting @samp{curl} there fails. The model is asked for the candidate labels@comma{} comma separated@comma{} most likely first. A name it returns is matched to the label it equals@comma{} and failing that to the shortest label it starts@comma{} so a name the model shortened still finds its project while a label that is itself the start of another still names its own. The candidates it names move to the front of the completion list in the order it gave them@comma{} each appearing once however often it is named@comma{} and the rest keep their original order behind them@comma{} so an answer that names only some projects@comma{} repeats one@comma{} or invents a label never hides a project. A request that fails is reported in the echo area and leaves the original order standing@comma{} so a ranking failure costs order rather than the whole action.

