The Claude backend options control account selection@comma{} session metadata@comma{} and non-interactive execution. @code{agent-claude-accounts} is an alist of account names to Claude configuration directories@comma{} either as bare paths or as plists with a @code{:home} and an optional @code{:pool} (@ref{Account model, , Account model})@comma{} and @code{agent-claude-account-file} stores the selected account across Emacs sessions. Use @code{agent-claude-select-account} or the shared account infix in @code{agent-menu} to switch accounts before starting a new session. Both commands are thin wrappers around @code{agent-account-select}@comma{} which persists the choice and syncs the account's config home; @code{agent-account-init} prepares or repairs any backend's account home.

Status polling is controlled by @code{agent-claude-status-interval}@comma{} which defaults to @samp{5} seconds and reads status JSON emitted by the bundled statusline script. A status file caught empty or half-written is skipped until the next poll. @code{agent-claude-interrupt-check-delay}@comma{} @samp{1.5} seconds by default@comma{} is how long after an escape the backend waits before checking the terminal for an interrupted turn (@ref{Session state machine, , Session state machine}). Usage polling is shared with the other backends (@ref{Account usage, , Account usage}); @code{agent-claude-usage-interval} and @code{agent-claude-usage-max-interval} are obsolete aliases of @code{agent-usage-interval} and @code{agent-usage-max-interval}. The Claude fetcher reads the account's OAuth token from the macOS Keychain@comma{} so accounts authenticated with an API key rather than an OAuth subscription—whose OAuth Keychain store is an empty JSON object—are silently skipped rather than treated as a broken lookup. The Keychain lookup runs from the temporary-file directory@comma{} so a poll that fires while the current buffer's @samp{default-directory} names a deleted worktree cannot break the subprocess spawn.

Batch execution options are passed to @samp{claude -p}. @code{agent-claude-batch-permission-mode} defaults to @samp{"auto"}@comma{} @code{agent-claude-batch-max-turns} defaults to @samp{30}@comma{} and the allowed tool@comma{} model@comma{} and appended-system-prompt options let you narrow or expand what non-interactive Claude tasks may do.

Four options that used to live here now belong to the backend-agnostic layer@comma{} because the workflows they configure work with either backend: @code{agent-claude-warn-kill-with-branches} is now @code{agent-warn-kill-with-branches} and @code{agent-claude-fork-worktree-directory} is now @code{agent-branch-worktree-directory} (@ref{Shared session behavior, , Shared session behavior})@comma{} while @code{agent-claude-log-directory} is now @code{agent-todo-log-directory} and @code{agent-claude-org-todo-in-progress-keyword} is now @code{agent-todo-in-progress-keyword} (@ref{Org TODO workflows, , Org TODO workflows}). Each old name remains as an obsolete alias@comma{} so an existing configuration keeps working and reports the new name.

@vindex agent-claude-settings-package-directory
The backend's status line and hooks reach each Claude session it starts through Claude Code's @samp{--settings} switch@comma{} so neither Claude Code sessions started outside Emacs nor the user's settings files are touched. While @code{agent-claude-mode} is on@comma{} every session started through @samp{claude-code--start} — which every interactive and resume path in @samp{claude-code} and this package uses — receives a @samp{--settings} file in @code{agent-claude-session-settings-directory} holding them; the non-interactive @samp{claude -p} runner gets none@comma{} since nothing observes its state. The file's name is a hash of its contents and it is never rewritten@comma{} so its commands always name the checkout loaded in this Emacs@comma{} and Emacs instances sharing the directory but loading different checkouts each pass their own; files unused for a week are deleted when the mode is enabled. Claude Code honors only the last @samp{--settings} switch@comma{} so one the user already passes@comma{} in @code{claude-code-program-switches} or a command's extra switches@comma{} as a file or as inline JSON@comma{} is removed and its settings are merged into the file instead: hook lists are combined@comma{} and a status line the user sets is kept unless the package owns it. Set @code{agent-claude-use-session-settings} to nil to pass nothing. Copies of these hooks in the user's own settings@comma{} written by earlier versions or by @code{agent-claude-setup-config}@comma{} would make each event arrive twice; @code{agent-claude-remove-global-config} removes them from @code{agent-claude-settings-file} and leaves everything else. It recognizes the package's commands by its bundled helpers under any checkout path@comma{} and the generic @samp{claude-code-hook-wrapper stop} command that earlier versions wrote only in that exact form@comma{} alone in its group. @code{agent-claude-setup-config} remains for anyone who wants the hooks in sessions started outside Emacs: @code{agent-claude-settings-file} names the settings file it and the individual ensure commands update. @code{agent-claude-statusline-script} points to the bundled statusline helper. @code{agent-claude-hook-wrapper}@comma{} the upstream @samp{claude-code} hook wrapper earlier versions ran as the @samp{Stop} hook@comma{} is obsolete: that hook waited for Emacs@comma{} so a stalled server held each turn open for up to Claude Code's ten-minute hook timeout. The bundled statusline and hook helpers are located in the package's source checkout@comma{} even when a package manager such as Elpaca loads byte-compiled files from a separate build directory that holds only the Lisp sources. @code{agent-claude-status-directory} controls where the helper writes per-buffer status JSON files. The statusline setup refreshes an existing agent-owned @samp{claude-code-statusline.sh} command@comma{} which keeps the settings file correct after the package moves or is renamed@comma{} while preserving unrelated custom Claude statusline commands. The turn-lifecycle setup likewise replaces any @samp{notify-emacs-state.sh} command under the same hook@comma{} so a rerun after switching checkouts leaves exactly one. Settings written this way still name a specific checkout and go stale when another one is loaded. Set @code{agent-claude-settings-package-directory} to a path through a symlink that follows the active configuration@comma{} and setup writes the helpers under that path instead; the session settings ignore it. Loading the backend does not modify Claude Code configuration. Each Claude session is started with @samp{EMACS_SOCKET_NAME} naming this Emacs's server socket@comma{} or @samp{EMACS_SERVER_FILE} for a TCP server@comma{} so the hooks' @samp{emacsclient} calls reach the Emacs that started the session even when several Emacs instances run servers. Every Claude session and @samp{claude -p} run is also started without the variables a Claude Code session sets for the processes it spawns (@samp{CLAUDECODE}@comma{} @samp{CLAUDE_PID}@comma{} and session markers such as @samp{CLAUDE_CODE_CHILD_SESSION} and @samp{CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID}). An Emacs started from inside a Claude Code session inherits them@comma{} and a session that kept them would count as part of the outer one; @samp{CLAUDE_CODE_CHILD_SESSION}@comma{} for one@comma{} turns off transcript saving. Configuration variables such as @samp{CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK} are passed on@comma{} and an IDE port another environment function sets@comma{} such as monet's @samp{CLAUDE_CODE_SSE_PORT}@comma{} is kept.

@node Codex backend
@section Codex backend

@vindex agent-codex-accounts
@vindex agent-codex-account-file
@vindex agent-codex-skill-directories
@vindex agent-codex-exec-approval-policy
@vindex agent-codex-exec-sandbox-mode
@vindex agent-codex-exec-skip-git-repo-check
The Codex backend options control account selection@comma{} skill discovery@comma{} and non-interactive execution. @code{agent-codex-accounts} is an alist of account names to Codex home directories@comma{} either as bare paths or as plists with a @code{:home} and an optional @code{:pool} (@ref{Account model, , Account model})@comma{} and @code{agent-codex-account-file} stores the selected account across Emacs sessions. Account homes keep credentials such as @samp{auth.json} and writable @samp{config.toml} settings separate. A missing account config is copied from @samp{~/.codex/config.toml} once; an existing config symlink is replaced with an independent copy. Canonical-home path references in these initial copies are rebased to the destination home; existing regular configs are preserved. Later preference and browser configuration changes stay local to each account@comma{} while canonical hook trust is copied to the account config for its shared hooks. Installed plugins are also account-local: existing plugin symlinks are materialized once so browser services resolve inside the account home trusted by Codex. Missing plugin directories are copied from the canonical home once; later plugin updates stay account-local. The canonical home itself is left unchanged. Shared Codex state such as @samp{hooks.json}@comma{} skills@comma{} sessions@comma{} and @samp{history.jsonl} is symlinked from @samp{~/.codex/} so hooks@comma{} skills@comma{} and chat history remain available after switching accounts; the Claude backend shares state between account homes the same way. When an account-local file conflicts with a shared item@comma{} it is backed up to a timestamped @samp{*.agent-backup-*} sibling before the symlink is created@comma{} so no local state is silently lost. Use @code{agent-codex-select-account} or the shared account infix in @code{agent-menu} to switch accounts before starting a new session. The infix asks for the backend explicitly even when the menu was opened in a session buffer@comma{} preventing a choice intended for one backend from changing another. Both commands persist the choice and sync the account's config home; @code{agent-account-init} prepares or repairs any backend's account home.

@code{agent-codex-skill-directories} is a list of additional directories scanned for Codex skills. The backend also scans standard Codex skill locations and project-local skill directories.

@code{agent-codex-exec-approval-policy}@comma{} @code{agent-codex-exec-sandbox-mode}@comma{} and @code{agent-codex-exec-skip-git-repo-check} configure the non-interactive Codex calls used by skill workflows. The approval policy is passed as a top-level Codex CLI option before the @samp{exec} subcommand@comma{} while the sandbox and repository-check options are passed to @samp{codex exec} itself.

The @samp{gptel} classifier requests used by backtrace debugging and project ranking ignore reasoning-block callback events and parse only the final response text. Backtrace debugging sends the model a truncated excerpt of the backtrace rather than the full contents: each line is cut at 400 characters and the excerpt is capped at 100@comma{}000 characters@comma{} since backtraces can embed multi-megabyte printed objects that exceed API payload limits@comma{} while the frame names at the start of each line suffice to identify packages. The agent session itself still receives the path to the complete backtrace file.

@node Chief-of-staff loop
@section Chief-of-staff loop

@vindex agent-chief-backend
@vindex agent-chief-mode
@vindex agent-chief-interval
@vindex agent-chief-directory
@vindex agent-chief-session-instance-name
@vindex agent-chief-state-file
@vindex agent-chief-context-functions
@vindex agent-chief-state-max-chars
@vindex agent-chief-max-turns
@vindex agent-chief-notify-function
@vindex agent-chief-record-model-state-updates
@vindex agent-chief-system-prompt
The optional @samp{agent-chief} module runs a periodic@comma{} auditable chief-of-staff workflow. Emacs owns the schedule@comma{} explicit state@comma{} and notifications. In the default @samp{session} mode@comma{} the selected backend is an ordinary long-lived Claude Code or Codex session that receives heartbeat prompts and remains available for normal conversation.

@code{agent-chief-backend} selects @samp{codex} or @samp{claude-code}. @code{agent-chief-mode} defaults to @samp{session}; set it to @samp{stateless} to use the older one-shot JSON-decision path. @code{agent-chief-interval} controls the timer interval in seconds. @code{agent-chief-directory} is the working directory for chief sessions and one-shot calls; it defaults to @code{user-emacs-directory}. @code{agent-chief-session-instance-name} defaults to @samp{chief} and is used as the instance name for the dedicated interactive session.

@code{agent-chief-state-file} is an Org file used as the durable@comma{} inspectable state store; @samp{agent-chief} never writes opaque memory. Day plans and manual notes are appended there and@comma{} when a chief session is live@comma{} submitted into that session as conversational context.

State context treats only a @samp{Plan YYYY-MM-DD} heading dated today as current. Older plan headings are supplied as historical context@comma{} not active obligations@comma{} unless restated in today's plan.

@code{agent-chief-record-model-state-updates} controls whether model-proposed state updates from stateless ticks are appended to the state file. It defaults to nil because periodic model observations are not user-approved durable memory. Use explicit notes and day plans for ordinary state updates.

@code{agent-chief-context-functions} is the extension point for agenda@comma{} task@comma{} calendar@comma{} project@comma{} or custom status snippets. Each function is called with no arguments and should return a string. Nil and empty strings are ignored. The state file is included separately@comma{} truncated to @code{agent-chief-state-max-chars} characters. @code{agent-chief-max-turns} limits Claude Code ticks; Codex ticks use the existing @samp{codex exec} settings from @samp{agent-codex}.

In session mode@comma{} @code{agent-chief-start-session} sends only a short conversational kickoff asking the model to ask for today's plan; it does not dump the state file or stateless JSON contract into the visible transcript. Heartbeat prompts ask the model to respond exactly @samp{No nudge.} when no contact is warranted@comma{} or @samp{Nudge: MESSAGE} when it should alert the user. The backend's ordinary notification event tells @samp{agent-chief} when the response is complete; @samp{agent-chief} then inspects the session text since the heartbeat and calls @code{agent-chief-notify-function} only for a nudge. The default notification function logs the message and uses @samp{alert.el} when available. @code{agent-chief-system-prompt} is the standing instruction that makes the loop selective and forbids inventing obligations not present in the supplied context.

In stateless mode@comma{} backend output may include echoed CLI transcript text before the answer. The chief loop scans that output and uses the last valid JSON object with a @samp{notify} field as the decision@comma{} so echoed response-schema examples do not confuse the parser.

