@node First-run setup
@section First-run setup

@findex agent-setup
@code{agent-setup} checks what the package needs outside Emacs and reports the result in the @samp{*agent-setup*} buffer. It looks for the @samp{claude} and @samp{codex} programs (@code{claude-code-program} and @code{codex-program}) and for @samp{emacsclient} on the @samp{exec-path}@comma{} and gives install instructions for any that are missing; it installs nothing itself. When Codex and @samp{agent-log} are installed@comma{} it also looks for @samp{timeout} or @samp{gtimeout}@comma{} which @samp{agent-log} needs to list Codex sessions and which macOS lacks; without one@comma{} its session browser and search silently show no Codex sessions. It offers to start the Emacs server when none is running@comma{} because the Claude Code hooks report session state through @samp{emacsclient}. It then writes the settings file passed to each Claude session and reports where it is (@ref{Claude Code backend, , Claude Code backend}). When @code{agent-claude-settings-file} still holds the package's status line or hooks@comma{} it offers to remove them with @code{agent-claude-remove-global-config}@comma{} since sessions would otherwise receive each event twice; declining leaves the file untouched and is reported@comma{} and a file without such entries is neither asked about nor written. Finally it offers to link the bundled skills into each tool's skills directory (@ref{Bundled skills, , Bundled skills}). Run the command again after fixing anything it marks as missing.

@node Bundled skills
@section Bundled skills

@vindex agent-setup-skill-directories
@vindex agent-setup-package-directory
The package ships three skills in @samp{etc/skills/}@comma{} each written for both Claude Code and Codex:

@itemize
@item
@samp{handoff} prepares the prompt for a fresh session and saves it where @code{agent-handoff} reads it. That command needs this skill@comma{} or one of your own that writes the same file.
@item
@samp{live-session-status} reports what another live session is doing by reading its transcript.
@item
@samp{message-agent-session} sends a message to another live session with @samp{bin/agent-send}.
@end itemize

@code{agent-setup} offers to link them into the directories in @code{agent-setup-skill-directories}@comma{} by default the @samp{skills} directories of the configuration homes Claude Code and Codex read: @samp{$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR} and @samp{$CODEX_HOME} when Emacs has them set@comma{} else @samp{~/.claude/} and @samp{~/.codex/}. It asks once per tool and links nothing without a yes. A tool whose configuration directory does not exist is skipped. A skill of the same name that the package did not link is yours and is never replaced. The links point into the source checkout@comma{} so updating the package updates the skills; a link left pointing at another checkout is offered for relinking. Set @code{agent-setup-package-directory} to a stable alias of the checkout when its real path changes@comma{} for example between Emacs profiles.

The executable @samp{bin/agent-send} submits a message@comma{} read from standard input@comma{} to another live session's buffer@comma{} and reports success only when the recipient's transcript records it. It prepends a header saying that the message comes from another agent session and was not typed by the user. @samp{agent-send --list} lists the live sessions@comma{} and @samp{agent-send --help} gives the full contract. Its library@comma{} @samp{lib/python/agent_session_lib.py}@comma{} holds the guarded prompt delivery and transcript checks@comma{} and other tools can load it by path.

@node Shared commands
@section Shared commands

@findex agent-menu
