@findex agent-setup-kill-on-exit
@findex agent-setup-scroll-keys
@findex agent-snippet-tab
The command @code{agent-menu} opens the unified transient menu. Every entry in it works with either backend: session switching@comma{} waiting-session jumps@comma{} resuming a past session@comma{} creating a branch of the current session@comma{} handoff@comma{} restart@comma{} exit@comma{} history browsing@comma{} skill execution@comma{} one dispatcher that acts on the thing at point@comma{} and a Prompts section beneath Tools in the same column for prompt capture and inserting captured prompts@comma{} one at a time or all at once.

The Sessions section holds general session management: new (@samp{n})@comma{} select
(@samp{s})@comma{} open a past session (@samp{o})@comma{} jump to the waiting session (@samp{w})@comma{} and log
(@samp{l}).
The Account section@comma{} at the bottom of the Options column@comma{} holds login (@samp{a l}) and usage (@samp{a u}).
The Current session column holds branch (@samp{b})@comma{} snooze or unsnooze (@samp{z})@comma{} handoff@comma{}
restart (@samp{r})@comma{} and exit commands@comma{} and beneath them a Monitor section for
tools outside Emacs that act on sessions: toggle monitor (@samp{m m})@comma{} open
review file (@samp{m f}) and process review file (@samp{m p}). Such a tool sets
@code{agent-review-file-function}@comma{} and the Monitor section appears only while
that option is set. It labels
@code{agent-exit} (@samp{x}) as “exit” and @code{agent-exit-without-skills} (@samp{X}) as “exit immediately”.

Tools use the @samp{t} prefix: run skill (@samp{t r}) and act on thing at point (@samp{t .}).
Prompts use @samp{p}: capture (@samp{p c})@comma{} insert one (@samp{p i})@comma{} and insert all (@samp{p a}).
Options put account selection first@comma{} then buffer protection and branch warnings@comma{}
followed by ready alerts@comma{} theme synchronization and hiding snoozed sessions (@samp{-z}).

@findex agent-history
The command @code{agent-history}@comma{} labeled “open log” and bound to @samp{l} in the Sessions column@comma{} opens the Agent Log menu for browsing@comma{} opening@comma{} searching@comma{} and resuming historical sessions across backends. Agent Log is an optional package: loading @samp{agent} never requires it@comma{} @code{agent-history} loads it on demand@comma{} and invoking the command without Agent Log installed signals a clear error. Neither @code{agent-menu} nor the session switcher reads any historical catalog while opening; catalog work (which for Codex can start an app-server process and take seconds) happens only after an explicit Agent Log action.

In EAT-backed Claude and Codex session buffers@comma{} @code{agent-setup-scroll-keys} makes PageUp and PageDown send terminal mouse-wheel events@comma{} matching the TUI scroll path used by the mouse. PageUp and PageDown in other selected buffers retain their normal bindings and never target a visible AI terminal in another window. Terminal helpers require buffer-local EAT state@comma{} so native app-server sessions cannot inherit a stale terminal from a killed buffer.

The command @code{agent-select-session} opens the session switcher@comma{} which contains only existing session entries. If no sessions are active@comma{} it reports that there are none; use @code{agent-start-new-session} (@samp{n}) to start one. Sessions receive stable home-row keys where possible and display backend icons next to their labels.

The switcher lists every session in one flat list ordered by home-row key@comma{} whatever account each session runs under. The account is a column of its own@comma{} between the session name and its annotation@comma{} shown dimmed in the @code{agent-session-account} face. A session is therefore found at the key it always answers to@comma{} rather than at a position that moves when a neighbor's account changes. Sessions whose backend has no accounts configured leave the column blank@comma{} and when no session on screen records an account the column is left out altogether.

A session that is waiting for something you can only do later can be set aside with @code{agent-toggle-snooze}@comma{} bound to @samp{z} in @code{agent-menu}; invoking it again unsnoozes. The menu labels the key @samp{snooze} or@comma{} when the session it was opened from is snoozed@comma{} @samp{unsnooze}. A snoozed session keeps its key and its buffer@comma{} but the switcher lists it after every active session in the dimmed italic @code{agent-snoozed} face instead of a state color@comma{} @code{agent-jump-to-waiting} skips it@comma{} and it fires no ready alert when its turn ends. When snoozing@comma{} the command reads a duration in hours from the minibuffer; leaving it blank snoozes indefinitely. A timed snooze ends by itself when the hours run out@comma{} and if the session is waiting for input at that point@comma{} the ready alert that snoozing suppressed fires then. The command acts on the current session@comma{} and prompts for one when the current buffer is not a session or with a prefix argument. The mark survives @code{agent-restart}@comma{} and resuming a session through @code{agent-restore-sessions} snoozes it again as it was when recorded; a timed snooze resumes with the time it had left@comma{} and one whose time ran out in the meantime is not restored. A handoff starts a new conversation@comma{} so it does not carry the mark over. The user option @code{agent-hide-snoozed-sessions}@comma{} a boolean that defaults to @code{t} and is toggled by @samp{-z} in @code{agent-menu}@comma{} leaves snoozed sessions out of the switcher and @code{agent-tab-bar-mode}'s segment altogether; they keep their keys and reappear when unsnoozed or when the option is turned off. While the option hides any session@comma{} the switcher ends with @samp{z} (“show snoozed sessions”)@comma{} which reopens it listing the snoozed sessions alone@comma{} each at its usual key and in its state color rather than the @code{agent-snoozed} face; @samp{z} there (“show active sessions”) goes back. The phone page still lists them.

@findex agent-toggle-wake-ups
@findex agent-session-accepts-wake-ups-p
A tool outside Emacs@comma{} such as a message monitor@comma{} can wake an idle session by submitting a prompt to it with @code{agent-submit}. Sessions refuse such wake-ups by default: @code{agent-toggle-wake-ups}@comma{} bound to @samp{m m} (“toggle monitor”) in @code{agent-menu}@comma{} lets the current session accept them@comma{} and invoking it again stops it. Tools ask with @code{agent-session-accepts-wake-ups-p}; agent.el does not enforce the setting on @code{agent-submit}@comma{} so a tool that never asks is not stopped by it. The command prompts for a session when the current buffer is not one or with a prefix argument. Like the snooze mark@comma{} the setting survives a restart and is restored with the session.

@vindex agent-review-file-function
@findex agent-open-review-file
Such a tool may also keep items for the user to review on a session's behalf@comma{} such as draft replies to the messages it routed there. Set @code{agent-review-file-function} to a function that takes the session buffer and returns that file (or nil when the session has none); @code{agent-open-review-file}@comma{} bound to @samp{m f} in the Monitor section of @code{agent-menu}@comma{} then opens it. Like @code{agent-toggle-wake-ups}@comma{} it acts on the current session and prompts for one when the current buffer is not a session or with a prefix argument. With the option unset@comma{} the command signals an error saying so.

@findex agent-process-review-file
@vindex agent-review-process-prompt
While a session accepts wake-ups@comma{} its doom-modeline status shows a small broadcast icon after the session name (the letter @samp{M} without Nerd Fonts); hovering over it explains it.

After writing comments in the review file@comma{} such as ``send''@comma{} ``change X'' or ``drop'' next to each draft@comma{} @code{agent-process-review-file} (@samp{m p}) hands them to the session: it saves the file if it has unsaved changes and submits @code{agent-review-process-prompt}@comma{} which asks the session to carry out every comment and then update the file. Since you invoke it@comma{} the prompt is your instruction@comma{} and the comments carry your authority.

@findex agent-tab-bar-mode
@vindex agent-tab-bar-refresh-interval
@vindex agent-tab-bar-key
@vindex agent-current-session
To see every session's state without opening the switcher@comma{} enable the global minor mode @code{agent-tab-bar-mode}. It adds the sessions to the tab bar@comma{} each preceded by its switcher key in the @code{agent-tab-bar-key} face@comma{} in the switcher's order and with its faces: @code{agent-waiting} (green)@comma{} @code{agent-waiting-with-background} (gold)@comma{} @code{agent-busy} (red)@comma{} @code{agent-unknown} and @code{agent-snoozed}. The phone page uses the same three colors for these states. The session a frame's selected window shows also carries the @code{agent-current-session} face in that frame@comma{} behind the state faces@comma{} so a frame's own session stands out whatever its state and two frames can each mark a different one. Clicking a session switches to it. The segment goes into @code{tab-bar-format} before @code{tab-bar-format-align-right}@comma{} which the mode adds when the format lacks it. Some states@comma{} such as background work@comma{} are read from files or the terminal rather than announced by an event@comma{} so the mode checks the sessions every @code{agent-tab-bar-refresh-interval} seconds (default @samp{1}) and redraws the tab bar when one changed. A change of selected window or of the buffer a window shows redraws it at once@comma{} through @code{window-selection-change-functions} and @code{window-buffer-change-functions}. The tab bar itself must be shown@comma{} for example with @code{tab-bar-mode}. Turning the mode off and on keeps the segment where it was among the other tab bar entries. Enabling the mode again while it is on restarts its refresh timer instead of adding a second one. An alignment entry the mode added stays while later entries rely on it.

@vindex agent-session-annotation-functions
Sessions can also carry a one-line annotation after their name@comma{} shown dimmed in the @code{agent-session-annotation} face. The text comes from @code{agent-session-annotation-functions}@comma{} an abnormal hook: each function is called with a session buffer and returns a string@comma{} or @code{nil} to leave the session to the functions after it. The first non-nil answer is the one shown@comma{} and the functions after it are not called. @samp{agent} renders whatever it returns and knows nothing about where the text comes from. Whitespace in the answer is collapsed to single spaces@comma{} so a multi-line answer cannot break the menu's layout@comma{} and an answer that is blank or not a string counts as no annotation. Sessions without an annotation — including sessions every function declines to annotate — render exactly as they did before annotations existed. The default is @code{nil}@comma{} which shows session names alone; Agent Log adds a function that supplies each session's stored one-line summary (@uref{https://github.com/benthamite/agent-log, agent-log})@comma{} and with no such integration loaded the switcher looks unchanged. Because this is a hook@comma{} an integration adds itself with @code{add-hook} rather than taking a slot over: a function you installed yourself keeps working whichever loads first@comma{} and @code{remove-hook} takes either one back out.

@vindex agent-session-state-summary-functions
@vindex agent-session-status-token-functions
@findex agent-session-set-status
@findex agent-session-state-summary
The switcher keeps the session summary and adds two columns after it: the
age of the current state and a short state description published by the agent.
Ages use seconds@comma{} minutes@comma{} hours@comma{} or days (@samp{12s}@comma{} @samp{2m}@comma{} @samp{1h}@comma{} @samp{3d})@comma{} measured
from the last observed state transition. Repeated events reporting the same
state do not reset the age; neither do changes to either summary. An
unobserved transition has no age. Ages refresh every five seconds while the
menu is open.

The state description comes from one of two sources. An agent can publish
one itself@comma{} for example “Testing pagination fix” or “Waiting for your
decision about bindings”@comma{} as described below. Without that@comma{} @samp{agent} derives
one from what the agent is doing@comma{} with no instruction to the agent@comma{} no model
call and no permission prompt:

@itemize
@item
Claude Code: the bundled @samp{record-activity.sh} hook@comma{} installed on
@samp{PreToolUse} for every session @samp{agent} starts (and by
@code{agent-claude-ensure-activity-hook-config}@comma{} part of
@code{agent-claude-setup-config})@comma{} records the to-do item in progress and each
tool call: the description Claude gives a shell command@comma{} or a plain
phrase such as “Reading init.el”. It answers at once and writes in the
background@comma{} one file per event in an @samp{.activity} directory keyed like the
statusline file@comma{} named by the time the event fired; @samp{agent} reads the
newest file of each kind and deletes the older ones@comma{} so a late write
never replaces a newer one. Subagents' tool calls are left out. The to-do
item in progress wins over tool calls while it belongs to the present
busy spell.
@item
Codex (app-server sessions): the plan step in progress; or else the
newer of the latest reasoning summary's heading and the latest item
Codex started@comma{} such as “Running make test” or “Editing init.el”; all
read from the notifications @samp{codex} passes to
@code{codex-app-server-notification-functions}.
@end itemize

@vindex agent-session-derived-status-functions
Backends add these through @code{agent-session-derived-status-functions}@comma{} an
abnormal hook called with a session buffer that returns a cons of the
activity's time and its text. A timer calls it every two seconds for busy
sessions@comma{} and once more when a session changes state@comma{} so the last activity
of a turn is caught; the result is cached@comma{} since the switcher and the mode
line must not read files. A published status wins while it is current. A
derived status is current while it describes the present busy spell@comma{} and
dims when the turn ends or the next one starts. When neither is current@comma{}
the more recent of the two is shown dimmed.

Until the session has done something or the agent has published@comma{} the
column is blank. A prompt submitted
from Emacs marks the previous description stale rather than clearing it:
it stays in the column in the face @code{agent-session-stale-status} (italic by
default) until the agent publishes again. Most prompts start turns too short
for the agent to publish in@comma{} so clearing the description on each one left
most sessions blank. A changed native session identity clears it@comma{} since it
belongs to another conversation. A turn that starts without a prompt@comma{} such
as a reply to a background-task notification@comma{} keeps it current@comma{} as does a
completed turn. Publication does not alter the state color or
its age@comma{} and a claim of completion remains the agent's report.

The executable @samp{bin/agent-status} accepts a status string of at most 160
characters (an empty string clears it). It routes through @samp{CODEX_BUFFER_NAME}
or @samp{CLAUDE_BUFFER_NAME}@comma{} requiring @samp{--backend codex} or
@samp{--backend claude-code} if both are present. Codex requires @samp{CODEX_THREAD_ID} to match the backend's current native
identity@comma{} including before the first submission has cached that identity.
A registered identity resolver is authoritative even when it returns no id;
only backends without one use their recorded identity. Claude requires @samp{AGENT_SESSION_UUID} to match its process token@comma{}
so an old process cannot update a replacement session with the same name. The helper
rejects absent or mismatched sessions@comma{} passes text as encoded JSON rather
than executable Lisp@comma{} and reports failures. When a briefly stalled Emacs
refuses the connection@comma{} the helper retries for about eight seconds before
reporting the failure. @samp{--socket-name} (or
@samp{EMACS_SOCKET_NAME}) selects a non-default Emacs server. Only the main agent
should publish status: subagents can inherit their parent's buffer variable.

Instruct agents to publish a factual phrase of roughly 5–12 words at task
start@comma{} meaningful phase changes@comma{} blockers@comma{} and before their final response.
Installing this instruction affects sessions that read it; already running
agents need to receive the instruction before they will publish updates.

The public function @code{agent-session-set-status} accepts a buffer name@comma{} backend
symbol@comma{} status text@comma{} optional native session id@comma{} and optional process token. The hook @code{agent-session-status-token-functions} supplies backend process tokens.
The function validates the target and text@comma{} stores the update in that buffer@comma{} and refreshes an open switcher.
@code{agent-session-state-summary-functions} supplies the column using the same
cheap@comma{} first-non-nil convention as the session-summary hook. The built-in
provider@comma{} @code{agent--session-status}@comma{} chooses between the published and the
derived status as described above@comma{} from cached values only.
The public function @code{agent-session-state-summary} returns a buffer's
current description as a single line@comma{} for display elsewhere such as the
mode line; publishing a status or marking it stale updates the mode line.

Names@comma{} accounts@comma{} and annotations each line up in one column: names are padded to the widest name in the menu and accounts to the widest account@comma{} measured in display columns@comma{} so a name containing double-width characters does not push the columns after it to the right. Padding that nothing follows is trimmed@comma{} so a session with no annotation ends at its account@comma{} and a session with neither ends at its name.

@vindex agent-session-annotation-max-width
The session summary and state-description columns are aligned across rows and
truncated with an ellipsis to fit the frame. After reserving the name@comma{}
account@comma{} graphical icon width@comma{} age@comma{} separators@comma{} and menu margins@comma{} each text
column can use half
the remaining width; space unused by a short update is available to the
session summary@comma{} and space unused by a short summary is available to the
update. @code{agent-session-annotation-max-width} caps only the session summary.
A zero cap hides that summary without hiding the age or state description.
When neither new column has data@comma{} the original annotation layout is used.


A function added to @code{agent-session-annotation-functions} must be cheap and free of side effects: building the switcher calls it more than once per session@comma{} because aligning the annotations needs every label's width before any label is final. Reading a value from memory is appropriate; reading a file or contacting a process is not.

The command @code{agent-run-skill} discovers skills from every loaded backend that implements skill discovery. It disambiguates same-named skills by backend label@comma{} prompts for declared arguments when a skill frontmatter requests them@comma{} and dispatches the invocation to the selected backend. Claude and Codex backends scan global and project-local programmatic skill directories@comma{} using both @code{project-current} and nearby tool directories as project-root signals@comma{} so batch-only skills can be run from this command without being loaded into ordinary interactive agent sessions.

The command @code{agent-capture-prompt} opens a session-specific Org file and appends a new prompt entry@comma{} leaving point below its heading@comma{} also when the file is already open in another window. The command @code{agent-insert-captured-prompt} reads pending nonempty entries for the current session@comma{} prompts for one with a short body preview in the completion candidate@comma{} inserts its text into the backend prompt without submitting it@comma{} and removes the selected Org entry from the capture file. With a prefix argument@comma{} it includes legacy entries already marked as inserted. The command @code{agent-insert-all-captured-prompts} inserts every pending prompt at once@comma{} as a numbered list@comma{} without submitting it@comma{} and removes their entries. Called from a capture file with an active region@comma{} it inserts only the prompts whose headings the region covers@comma{} into that file's session@comma{} and switches to it; a capture file is recognized by its location under @code{agent-prompt-capture-directory}@comma{} so this works however the file was opened. When a session still has pending captured prompts@comma{} @code{agent-exit}@comma{} @code{agent-exit-without-skills}@comma{} @code{agent-restart}@comma{} and @code{agent-handoff} ask for confirmation before closing or replacing that session.

The command @code{agent-act-on-slack-message}@comma{} called on a message in a Slack room
buffer@comma{} starts a session in the project that message is about and inserts its
permalink into the prompt without submitting it. With a prefix argument it
inserts the permalink into a running session instead. Its extractor@comma{}
@code{agent-slack-context}@comma{} answers with the message text and no directory@comma{} since a
message names no project of its own: the project is read from the account's
sources and ranked by that text (@ref{Project sources, , Project sources}).

A message that contains a Claude Code resume command@comma{} @samp{claude --resume} followed
by a session id@comma{} is handled differently: it names a session@comma{} not a task. Such a
message is typically an alert reply posted for a background session that has
stopped to ask for a decision. It is a thing at point of its own@comma{} checked before
an ordinary Slack message@comma{} so @code{agent-act-on-thing-at-point} asks for no backend
and no project: the command switches to that session's buffer when one is live@comma{}
and otherwise resumes the session in a new buffer in the directory its
transcript records@comma{} so the conversation continues where it stopped. The prefix
argument makes no difference here. A resume command whose transcript cannot be
found signals an error rather than starting a new session.

The command @code{agent-act-on-forge-notification} is the Forge counterpart of
@code{agent-act-on-slack-message}. Called on a notification in
@code{forge-list-notifications}@comma{} it starts a session in the working tree of the
repository that notification belongs to and inserts the issue or pull request
URL into the prompt without submitting it. It also accepts the topic at point@comma{}
so it works from a topic buffer or a Magit status buffer@comma{} and with a prefix
argument it sends the URL to a running session instead of starting one. Not
every notification is about a topic: Github also notifies about check suites@comma{}
commits and releases@comma{} and Forge records no topic for those. Such a notification
is routed as itself@comma{} because it names a repository to work in and a URL to
read; its title goes into the prompt with the URL@comma{} since the URL of a failed
workflow run names the page listing every run of the repository and the title
is what says which one this is. The
command is a thin wrapper over the routing layer described below: its
extractor@comma{} @code{agent-forge-context}@comma{} reads the subject at point and answers with a
context suggesting the working tree@comma{} so the project reader opens on that clone
already selected: a bare @samp{RET} starts there@comma{} and the same prompt is where a
different project is chosen instead. Routing a topic therefore ends in the same
choice as routing a Slack message or a backtrace@comma{} rather than starting a session
outright.

A Slack message has to be classified to find its project@comma{} whereas a Forge
notification already records its repository. Where that repository is cloned is
a second question@comma{} and it is asked in three steps. Forge answers first@comma{} from the
worktree it recorded; it records one only for a repository it has seen from a
local clone@comma{} and it forgets one whose path stopped being a worktree@comma{} which is
what moving a repository does to every path at once. The account's project
sources answer next@comma{} with @code{agent-project-repository-directory}: they are where
the clones live@comma{} so they know the current path of a repository that moved and
the path of one Forge never saw. When neither knows@comma{} the context carries no
directory and the core reads a project from its text@comma{} which leads with the
repository's slug so that ranking puts the right project first. Nothing is
refused for want of a clone.

The command @code{agent-act-on-email}@comma{} called on a message in a mu4e headers or
view buffer@comma{} starts a session in the project that message is about and inserts
the message's maildir path into the prompt without submitting it. With a
prefix argument it sends the path to a running session instead. The payload is
the path rather than the text of the mail because an agent can open that file
itself and read the headers@comma{} the body and any attachments from it@comma{} which no
excerpt pasted into a prompt would carry. Like Slack routing and unlike Forge
routing@comma{} the context names no directory: a message belongs to no project of its
own@comma{} so its extractor@comma{} @code{agent-mu4e-context}@comma{} answers with the subject and the
sender as the text the project is ranked against (@ref{Project sources, , Project sources}).

The sender comes from the message's @samp{:from} field@comma{} which @samp{mu} returns as a
list of @samp{(:email EMAIL :name NAME)} plists — one per sender@comma{} with the name
absent when the sender gave none — and reads as @samp{Name <address>}@comma{} or as the
bare address when there is no name. Like its Slack and Forge counterparts@comma{} the
command loads its foreign package itself and says so when it is missing@comma{} since
the command is autoloaded and can therefore be called from a buffer that has
never loaded mu4e.

It signals an error when the message records no path — mu4e reports a missing
string field as the empty string rather than as nil@comma{} so an empty path counts as
no path — and when the recorded file cannot be read. A maildir file name
encodes the message's flags@comma{} so reading a message@comma{} or another mail program
touching it@comma{} renames the file@comma{} and mu's index can name a path that no longer
exists until it is reindexed. As with a stale Forge clone path@comma{} that is an
ordinary failure@comma{} and catching it here is better than handing the agent a path
it cannot open.

@findex agent-act-on-linear-issue
The command @code{agent-act-on-linear-issue} routes a Linear issue mirrored by
@samp{org-linear}@comma{} from its heading in the mirrored file or from the agenda line
showing it@comma{} such as one in the list @code{org-linear-my-issues} builds. @samp{org-linear}
writes each issue as an org TODO heading filed under a level-1 heading named
after its Linear project@comma{} with the issue's URL in a @samp{LINEAR_URL} property@comma{} so
the issue is routed as the org TODO it is@comma{} except that its heading and body
are typed into the prompt of the session started for it rather than submitted@comma{}
since an issue is written for the people tracking it rather than as a prompt and
usually wants editing first. The heading moves to
@code{agent-todo-in-progress-keyword}@comma{} which @samp{org-linear} then pushes to Linear as
a state change. Three more things are added. When the account has a project whose
directory is named like the Linear project@comma{} ignoring case and separators@comma{} so
that @samp{White glove} finds @samp{white-glove}@comma{} that directory is suggested@comma{} so the
project reader opens on it and a bare @samp{RET} starts there@comma{} as a Forge topic's
clone is suggested; @code{agent-project-named-directory} does the lookup. Otherwise
the Linear project's name leads the text the project is ranked by@comma{} since it is
the likeliest clue to the repository the issue is about; an issue in no project
is ranked by its own text alone. And the issue's URL follows the prompt@comma{} so the
agent can reach the issue itself. The
origin of an issue is @samp{linear:} followed by the workspace slug of its URL@comma{} so an
entry in @code{agent-account-origin-rules} can tie a Linear workspace to an account.
Its extractor@comma{} @code{agent-org-linear-context}@comma{} reads only the properties and outline
@samp{org-linear} writes@comma{} so nothing of @samp{org-linear} is loaded.

@findex agent-act-on-thing-at-point
@vindex agent-at-point-things
The menu binds no routing command directly. @code{agent-act-on-thing-at-point}@comma{} bound to @samp{t .} in the Tools column@comma{} walks @code{agent-at-point-things} and runs the extractor of the first entry whose predicate matches the current buffer: a backtrace in a @samp{*Backtrace*} buffer@comma{} a Slack message in a Slack room buffer@comma{} a Forge notification or topic@comma{} an email in a mu4e headers or view buffer@comma{} a Linear issue mirrored by @samp{org-linear}@comma{} or an org TODO heading. The last two are read from an org buffer or from an agenda line@comma{} whose entry is the heading the line shows; the Linear issue comes first because it is an org TODO too. Every predicate answers from the buffer alone — the buffer name@comma{} the buffer-local variable @samp{slack} sets on room buffers@comma{} the major mode@comma{} the org TODO state@comma{} the @samp{LINEAR_URL} property — so dispatch never loads a module to ask whether it applies@comma{} and the Forge and mu4e lookups run only in a buffer whose major mode can hold what they look for. A Magit buffer is one of those@comma{} and it can be current with @samp{forge} not yet loaded; since forge's lookups are not autoloaded@comma{} the Forge predicate also checks that they are defined@comma{} and falls through to the next thing rather than failing when they are not. The predicate claims any notification@comma{} not only one about a topic@comma{} because the extractor routes the notification itself when Forge records no topic for it. The mu4e modes need no such check@comma{} because the files that define those modes require the one that defines the lookup. When nothing matches@comma{} the command signals an error naming those six kinds@comma{} in the order the table holds them.

Recognizing the thing@comma{} choosing the session@comma{} and delivering the payload are three separate layers. An extractor takes a continuation rather than returning a value@comma{} because extraction can require a network round-trip@comma{} and calls it with a context plist: @samp{:directory} when the thing carries a working directory@comma{} as an absolute path@comma{} @samp{:suggested-directory} when it worked one out and the user should still be asked@comma{} @samp{:text} when it carries only text to choose a project from@comma{} @samp{:payload} for what to send@comma{} @samp{:submit} for whether to submit it@comma{} and an optional @samp{:after} thunk run once the payload is delivered. Before extraction begins@comma{} the core resolves the backend and captures that backend's selected account as one pair. A later account switch cannot change which project sources this invocation reads@comma{} which matters for asynchronous extractors such as Slack. A context that names a directory starts its session there; one that suggests a directory@comma{} or names none at all@comma{} has its project read from the captured account's sources with @code{agent-project-read}@comma{} led by the suggestion or ranked by the text. With a prefix argument the payload goes to a running session chosen by completion instead of a new one@comma{} so the same key routes a thing either way.

A running session already has a directory@comma{} so on that path the core tells the extractor not to resolve one@comma{} and an extractor that has to work for its directory leaves it out. That saves what the work costs: routing a backtrace to a running session asks no model which packages it implicates and prompts for none@comma{} and routing a Forge topic there no longer refuses a repository that was never cloned locally. The extractors that carry only text are unaffected@comma{} since the project they would otherwise pick is chosen after the session is@comma{} and only for a new one.

What each kind sends differs@comma{} because the payloads differ. A Slack permalink@comma{} a Forge topic URL and an email's maildir path are references to something the agent should read@comma{} so they arrive unsubmitted and you type the request next to them. A backtrace and an org TODO already are the request@comma{} so they submit. A Linear issue is a request too@comma{} but one written for the people tracking it@comma{} so it arrives unsubmitted for you to edit.

A payload marked @samp{:submit} reaches a @strong{new} session as its initial prompt@comma{} handed to the backend at launch rather than typed into the terminal afterwards. Claude passes it on the CLI's command line@comma{} and Codex does too except when it is resuming a session or running through its app server@comma{} where it sends the prompt itself once the session is up. Either way the payload never races the terminal: nothing sequences typed input against a session whose process has only just been spawned@comma{} and these sessions often start in a directory the CLI has never opened@comma{} where it greets the user with a trust prompt that the typed text would answer instead of the model. A payload the context does not submit is typed in on both paths@comma{} since it is meant to sit in the prompt for the user to edit. A submitted payload reaching an already-running session is typed in and then submitted@comma{} because that session is past its startup and needs no hand-off at launch.

Because extraction can be asynchronous@comma{} the continuation may run long after the command was invoked@comma{} in whatever buffer the user has moved to since. The dispatcher therefore captures the invocation buffer and resolves the backend up front@comma{} before the extractor runs: the session adopts the backend of the buffer the thing was read from rather than of wherever the callback lands@comma{} no backend prompt appears in the middle of an asynchronous wait@comma{} and the @samp{:after} thunk runs back in the invocation buffer@comma{} which starting a session has displaced as the current one. A thunk whose buffer has been killed meanwhile is dropped@comma{} since the thing it would act on is gone.

The commands @code{agent-handoff}@comma{} @code{agent-debug-backtrace}@comma{} @code{agent-act-on-slack-message}@comma{} @code{agent-exit}@comma{} and @code{agent-restart} resolve a backend from the current session buffer or by prompting. This lets the same keybinding operate on Claude or Codex sessions without separate wrapper commands. The old per-backend command names (@code{agent-claude-handoff}@comma{} @code{agent-codex-restart}@comma{} and so on) remain available as obsolete aliases. @code{agent-exit}@comma{} bound to @samp{x}@comma{} kills the session buffer after any configured before-exit skills finish. It snoozes the session first@comma{} as @code{agent-toggle-snooze} does@comma{} so that the session stays out of the switcher's active list and fires no ready alert while that closeout runs; declining the captured-prompts confirmation leaves the session unsnoozed. @code{agent-exit-without-skills}@comma{} bound to @samp{X}@comma{} uses the same exit path but inhibits the configured before-exit skills. It still checks for pending captured prompts and runs other before-exit functions. Neither command deletes the Emacs window that displayed the session.

@code{agent-handoff} reads the backend's entry in @code{agent-handoff-files}@comma{} kills the current session buffer@comma{} and starts a new session with the handoff prompt. By default the new session keeps the source directory. A handoff file can select another directory with YAML front matter:

@example
---
target-directory: ../other-project
---

Continue from this handoff.
@end example

The target can be absolute or relative to the source session directory@comma{} and it must name an existing directory. @code{agent-handoff} resolves and validates it before it kills the source session. Lisp callers can pass the optional @code{TARGET-DIRECTORY} argument instead; an explicit argument takes precedence over file metadata@comma{} and file metadata takes precedence over the source directory.

@code{agent-handoff-from-emacsclient} accepts the source buffer name as its first @samp{emacsclient} argument and the optional target directory as its second argument. This lets agent-authored handoff commands close the requesting session even though Emacs evaluates the form outside that buffer. If the source buffer name is unavailable@comma{} handoff closes the single existing session buffer for the source directory before starting the replacement session@comma{} which avoids the CLI's instance-name prompt in unattended loops.

@code{agent-restart} kills the current session buffer and resumes the same session in place@comma{} preserving the session's directory@comma{} instance name@comma{} any launch state the backend's @code{:restart-options} function captures@comma{} and the state set on it: its snooze@comma{} whether it accepts wake-ups@comma{} and its switcher key. It reroutes sessions within the selected account pool without prompting. Otherwise it prompts when the active account differs from the session account@comma{} and it leaves the account unset when neither the session nor the global selection names one@comma{} so a session that ran under the backend's ambient default home is restarted there rather than pushed onto an account it never used.  Every precondition is checked before the old session is killed: restart refuses when a named account is no longer configured@comma{} when the account is logged out@comma{} or when no resumable session identity exists@comma{} and in each case the running session survives untouched. @code{agent-audit-project} prompts for a project directory and runs each skill in @code{agent-audit-skills} sequentially through the backend's non-interactive prompt runner. @code{agent-debug-backtrace} saves the current Emacs backtrace@comma{} asks @samp{gptel} to identify likely packages@comma{} and submits instructions to fix the bug to a session started in the selected package source directory@comma{} or to a running session under a prefix argument. Its extractor@comma{} @code{agent--backtrace-context}@comma{} schedules the identification to run after the current command rather than doing it inline@comma{} because saving the backtrace kills the @samp{*Backtrace*} buffer@comma{} which exits the debugger's recursive edit and unwinds the calling frame. @code{agent-setup-kill-on-exit} arranges for a session buffer to be killed when its process exits@comma{} consulting the backend's @code{:before-kill-check} slot first. A process that exits with a non-zero code keeps its buffer@comma{} with a message@comma{} because the buffer holds the error that explains the failure — a CLI that cannot start would otherwise vanish without a trace; a process ended by a signal is killed as usual. It is not a menu entry: each backend installs it@comma{} along with @code{agent-disable-scrollback-truncation} and@comma{} for Codex@comma{} @code{agent-fix-rendering}@comma{} from its session-start hook@comma{} so the menu carries no buffer-maintenance commands.

@code{agent-restore-sessions}@comma{} bound to @samp{g r} in the Session groups section@comma{} is the one command that resumes a saved set of sessions. It offers the session groups saved with @code{agent-save-session-group} (below) and@comma{} as @samp{Previous Emacs}@comma{} the sessions that were live when the last Emacs died@comma{} whether it quit@comma{} crashed@comma{} or went down with the machine. Each choice is annotated with its session count and time. Each Emacs records its live sessions in @code{agent-session-registry-file} whenever a session starts@comma{} learns its native id@comma{} or ends@comma{} so the record survives deaths that skip Emacs's exit hooks; batch Emacs records nothing. An Emacs counts as dead when no process has its pid and start time@comma{} because a reboot reuses pids. An Emacs that @code{restart-emacs} replaced counts as dead too@comma{} although its successor runs in the same process@comma{} because each Emacs also records when it started up. The first save after a restart turns the sessions of every dead Emacs into the restorable set@comma{} replacing the previous one even when the dead Emacs had none@comma{} so the previous Emacs's sessions are offered only in the Emacs that follows it and do not pile up. Choosing them resumes each session with its backend@comma{} account@comma{} directory@comma{} instance@comma{} session id@comma{} the launch state its backend's @code{:restart-options} captured@comma{} and the state set on it: its snooze@comma{} whether it accepts wake-ups@comma{} and its switcher key. A session gets its key back unless a session outside the restored set holds it@comma{} in which case it gets the first free key. The record is rewritten whenever a snooze or wake-up setting changes too@comma{} so it reflects the session as it last was. It skips sessions already live and keeps failed ones recorded so it can be run again. When a live session has since taken a recorded session's instance name in the same directory@comma{} the recorded one resumes under the first free name derived from it@comma{} such as @samp{default-1}; @code{agent-start-session} itself refuses an instance name a live session holds@comma{} since the backend would otherwise hand back that session's buffer and relabel it with the new identity. A session is recorded only once its native id is known@comma{} so one killed before its first status update cannot be restored.

Resuming a Claude session whose transcript records no messages@comma{} by this command or any other@comma{} resumes the session that continued in it instead@comma{} because that one holds the conversation. Claude Code leaves such a transcript@comma{} with only title records@comma{} when it continues a session under a new id and no turn follows. The echo area names both sessions. A session of that id still running in the background is attached to as usual.

@code{agent-save-session-group}@comma{} bound to @samp{g s}@comma{} saves every live resumable session under a name in @code{agent-session-groups-file}@comma{} recording each one as the registry above does; saving under an existing name replaces that group after confirmation. It then offers to close the saved sessions@comma{} exiting each as @code{agent-exit-without-skills} does@comma{} so no before-exit skills run. Restoring a group through @code{agent-restore-sessions} resumes its sessions@comma{} skipping those already live. Each comes back as it was saved@comma{} snoozed@comma{} accepting wake-ups and on its switcher key as the registry restores them. The group is kept@comma{} so it can be restored again. @code{agent-delete-session-group}@comma{} bound to @samp{g d}@comma{} removes a saved group. The three commands sit in their own Session groups section of the menu@comma{} beneath Sessions. @code{agent-restore-session-group}@comma{} which restored only groups@comma{} is obsolete.

@code{agent-before-exit-functions} can delay @code{agent-exit} for cleanup work.
Each function receives the resolved backend and session buffer; returning nil
aborts the exit.
When a turn is active@comma{} Agent waits for it to finish before it submits the first
before-exit skill@comma{} so the skill cannot steer or abort the user's turn.  A
backend that reports both a stop and a later idle-prompt notification for one
turn advances the chain only once.
Once a forced session close proceeds@comma{} Agent kills the target buffer without
running buffer-local kill-query prompts@comma{} so stale session buffers do not survive
restart or exit workflows.

Set @code{agent-before-exit-skill-name} (a single skill) or
@code{agent-before-exit-skill-names} (an ordered list) to submit interactive skills
before exiting matching sessions. Each entry in @code{agent-before-exit-skill-names}
is either a skill-name string or a list whose car is the skill name and whose
cdr is a plist accepting @code{:directories} (restrict where that skill runs@comma{}
overriding @code{agent-before-exit-skill-directories}) and @code{:args} (a string
appended to the submitted command). Set @code{:receipt} non-nil when the skill must
confirm its closeout result before the chain can continue. For example:

@lisp
(setq agent-before-exit-skill-names
      '("session-learning-capture"
        ("update-log" :directories ("~/work")
                      :args "--auto" :receipt t)))
@end lisp

@code{agent-run-skill-before-exit} uses each backend's registered skill command
prefix so Claude sessions receive slash commands and Codex sessions receive
dollar-prefixed skill commands@comma{} then submits the first applicable skill. The
skills run in order@comma{} each submitted after the previous one finishes@comma{} and the
session exits after the last. These submissions do not display the session@comma{} so
an exit chain continues in the background after the user switches away;
ordinary ready notifications are left unchanged while the chain runs. When
@code{agent-before-exit-skill-names} is nil@comma{} @code{agent-before-exit-skill-name} is used
as a single-skill fallback. Leave @code{agent-before-exit-skill-directories} nil to
run an unscoped skill before exiting every session.

For a @code{:receipt} entry@comma{} Agent creates a private temporary directory and appends
@code{--receipt-file PATH} to the submitted command. The skill must write one JSON
object to that path. Its @code{status} must be exactly @code{success}@comma{} @code{no-op}@comma{} @code{held}@comma{}
or @code{failure}; a @code{held} or @code{failure} receipt can include a @code{message}. Agent
advances the chain or exits only after @code{success} or @code{no-op}. @code{held} means the
skill finished its own work but needs the user@comma{} for example to approve
proposals it presented: the chain stops without a warning@comma{} the session stays
open and is marked as awaiting input (with the usual ready alert)@comma{} and the echo
area names the skill and shows the receipt's @code{message}. A missing file@comma{} invalid
JSON@comma{} unknown status@comma{} or @code{failure} stops the chain@comma{} keeps the session open@comma{} and
shows a warning; a missing file is ignored while the backend's idle probe still shows
the session working@comma{} since that completion event belongs to an earlier turn. It
is also ignored while the turn that just ended left background work running@comma{}
such as a subagent or a background shell the skill is waiting on: the skill
resumes when that work reports back and writes its receipt in a later turn. The
Claude backend reads that work from the @code{background_tasks} list of the Stop
hook payload@comma{} and Codex from its footer. After @code{held} or
@code{failure}@comma{} the next @code{agent-exit} starts the chain again from its first entry.
Agent removes the temporary receipt directory after reading it or
tearing down the session. Receipt gating is per entry: plain entries keep the
stop-event behavior. This lets a learning-capture entry run independently
before a receipt-gated bookkeeping entry. Repeating @code{agent-exit} while a chain
is active does not bypass it; it reports the chain's state in the echo area@comma{}
and @code{agent-exit-without-skills} is the explicit close that skips configured
skills. When @code{agent-exit} is called on a session that is still responding@comma{} the
chain arms in a waiting state and starts its first skill on the next stop
event; a message says so at arming time@comma{} since nothing else is visible until
the turn ends.

@code{agent-exit} runs every applicable configured skill regardless of session
duration. @code{agent-exit-without-skills} is the only exit command that bypasses
the skill hook; it still runs other before-exit functions.

A session with no native id on which no session event has ever arrived has not
started a conversation. That is what a CLI started with a bare @samp{--resume} looks
like while it shows its picker@comma{} which would take submitted text as a search and
never exit. Both exit commands therefore submit nothing to such a session: the
chain does not start@comma{} and the process is killed instead of receiving @samp{/exit}.
Both conditions are required@comma{} so a real session whose first status poll is slow
keeps its skills@comma{} because the prompt that started its turn has already raised an
event. Only one narrow case goes the other way: pressing @samp{RET} in the picker
counts as a prompt@comma{} so if that leaves the picker on screen@comma{} the session is treated
as started.

The chain is driven by session stop events and guarded by a watchdog. Each
submitted skill gets @code{agent-before-exit-timeout} seconds (default 600) before
the watchdog looks at it@comma{} and the watchdog restarts when the next skill begins.
If the current skill exceeds that interval@comma{} the watchdog warns but keeps the
exit request armed and looks again after another interval. The chain checks the
result when the skill eventually finishes instead of losing the requested exit
because the skill ran for more than ten minutes. A gated entry still needs an
accepted receipt at that point.

A stop event can be lost@comma{} and a chain that waited only for it would then stall
with the session sitting idle. So each time the watchdog fires it also asks
whether the skill has in fact finished. A running chain advances only when two
independent sources agree: the backend's @code{:idle-p} probe@comma{} which for Claude reads
the screen@comma{} and its @code{:reported-idle-p} source@comma{} which for Claude is a current
@samp{idle} reading from @samp{claude agents --json}. A session Claude Code reports as
@samp{waiting} does not count@comma{} since it is blocked on the user@comma{} as at a permission
dialog. A backend without both sources@comma{} such as Codex@comma{} keeps waiting for its stop
event. Advancing this way goes through the same step as a real stop event@comma{} so a
gated entry with no receipt at that point stops the chain with a warning and
keeps the session open rather than closing it.

Backends can veto that automatic close while the submitted skill invocation is
still visible as unaccepted prompt input. Codex uses this to avoid closing a
session when the dollar-prefixed skill name was inserted but not submitted@comma{}
while ignoring submitted command scrollback@comma{} visible placeholder text@comma{} and
autosuggestion text. The readiness check recognises the standard Codex prompt
markers (@samp{›} and @samp{❯}); a chain that stalls after the first skill usually means
the running Codex renders an unrecognised prompt marker.

A chain stays armed across stop events until its queue drains@comma{} so a chain
whose command was typed but never accepted would otherwise fire its next skill
into whatever the user does in that buffer later. A prompt the user sends from
the terminal therefore cancels any armed chain in that buffer@comma{} with a message.
Claude reports such prompts as a @code{user-submit} session event from its Return
key paths; Codex's submission hook runs for programmatic sends too@comma{} so it
reports plain @code{submit} events and cannot cancel a chain this way.

Backends can provide an atomic submit operation for before-exit commands.
Claude and Codex both target the resolved session buffer directly@comma{} insert the
skill command@comma{} and submit it before the terminal buffer is displayed again.
Codex delegates this to @samp{codex.el}'s target-buffer submit helper. Claude
inserts the command@comma{} waits @code{agent-claude-submit-delay} seconds with
@code{sleep-for} (pending keyboard input cannot shorten it@comma{} as it would a
@code{sit-for}@comma{} and a Return that arrives too soon is folded into the inserted text
as a newline)@comma{} and sends Return. It then checks the prompt line and re-sends
Return up to @code{agent-claude-submit-retries} times while the command is still
sitting there unsubmitted@comma{} since Claude Code occasionally swallows the first
one; the prompt marker's trailing space is a no-break space under @samp{eat}.

@node Phone remote control
@section Phone remote control

@findex agent-remote-mode
@findex agent-restart-with-account
