@vindex agent-remote-new-session-directory
The global minor mode @code{agent-remote-mode}@comma{} in @samp{agent-remote.el}@comma{} serves a touch-friendly control page so a phone can watch and unblock sessions. The page lists every live session with its state@comma{} backend@comma{} account and time in that state@comma{} waiting sessions first. The bar above the list holds@comma{} from left to right@comma{} New@comma{} the sort criterion with its direction arrow@comma{} and Report. The criterion keeps the list sorted by project (the session directory's name)@comma{} account@comma{} last change or status (waiting@comma{} background work@comma{} unknown@comma{} busy)@comma{} with ties going to the latest change; the arrow button beside it reverses the order. Manual@comma{} the default@comma{} shows waiting sessions first and keeps any order the cards were dragged into. Dragging a card while the list is sorted switches to Manual with the order as shown. The phone remembers the choice. The backend is shown by the icon Emacs uses for it@comma{} which the page reads from the backend's @code{:icon-svg} slot through @samp{/api/icons}; a backend without one is named in text. Snoozed sessions come below a dividing line@comma{} dimmed and with a grey edge. Swiping a card left snoozes or unsnoozes the session through @code{agent-toggle-snooze}; a snooze from the phone is indefinite. Swiping it right opens the Exit sheet. Long-pressing a card and dragging it moves it within its section. A section you have dragged keeps that order@comma{} with new sessions at its top@comma{} instead of sorting by state. The order is kept in the page's local storage@comma{} so it belongs to that phone or browser and does not change the session keys or the tab bar. Tapping a session shows the last @code{agent-remote-output-lines} lines of its buffer@comma{} refreshed every 1.5 seconds. A text box submits a reply through @code{agent-submit}@comma{} and the keys on show follow what the session is doing. Each session keeps its own draft: what you type in one session's box stays with that session when you open another@comma{} comes back when you return to it@comma{} and is cleared once it is sent. Drafts last as long as the page's tab@comma{} including the reload that follows an update. While the session's screen shows a dialog or picker in place of its input box@comma{} Emacs refuses the reply and the draft stays in the box: the dialog would take the text as keystrokes@comma{} so its first characters would answer or dismiss the dialog and only the rest would reach the prompt. Answer or dismiss the dialog with its keys@comma{} then send. At the input box there are none. While a turn runs@comma{} a Stop button in the status line sends escape. A dialog's or picker's card has up@comma{} down@comma{} return and escape@comma{} and a button for each numbered option on it. The @samp{···} button in the status line opens all the rest over the conversation@comma{} grouped by purpose: escape and @samp{C-c} to interrupt@comma{} up@comma{} down and return to move through a menu@comma{} the digits 1 to 3 and @samp{y} and @samp{n} to pick an option@comma{} and shift-tab and tab to cycle modes. It stays closed unless the session's backend keeps no transcript@comma{} where it starts open@comma{} and a session without a terminal@comma{} such as a Codex session run through its app server@comma{} has neither the button nor the keys. An escape goes through the backend's @samp{:interrupt} function@comma{} @code{claude-code-send-escape} for Claude and Codex's own escape action for Codex@comma{} so an interrupted turn is detected as it would be from Emacs@comma{} and a Codex app-server session@comma{} which has no terminal@comma{} can still be stopped. Because the page drives the session buffers themselves@comma{} it reaches Claude and Codex sessions under every account. Keys need a terminal session; a session without an EAT terminal accepts only text.

A Claude session runs on its terminal's alternate screen@comma{} so its buffer holds one screenful and no scrollback; the rest of the conversation exists only in the session's transcript. For a session with a transcript the page therefore makes the transcript the main view@comma{} fetched from @samp{/api/history} and shown as messages down to the bottom. Each refresh passes the byte offset it has reached to @samp{/api/output} and receives what the transcript has gained since@comma{} which costs one file status check while nothing is new. A session with no prompt yet has no transcript@comma{} so it opens as an empty conversation@comma{} and the conversation appears within seconds of the first prompt. A refresh leaves the scroll position alone@comma{} and stays at the bottom if the view is at the bottom. Scrolling to the top@comma{} or tapping Load older@comma{} fetches the preceding stretch. One request reads at most @code{agent-remote-history-bytes} bytes@comma{} whatever the transcript's size@comma{} at about 12 ms per MiB@comma{} because the request runs on Emacs's main thread. People's text is shown as messages exactly as typed@comma{} keeping its whitespace@comma{} and the model's with its Markdown rendered: emphasis@comma{} inline and fenced code@comma{} headings@comma{} lists@comma{} block quotes@comma{} links and tables@comma{} with wide code and tables scrolling sideways by themselves. The page builds these from text nodes and never from markup@comma{} code is not parsed further@comma{} and only http and https addresses become links; anything malformed stays readable as text. A message renders once@comma{} when it arrives. A run of tool calls becomes one line of tool names@comma{} and thinking@comma{} tool input and tool output are left out. A Codex session gets the same view from its rollout file@comma{} through @code{agent-util-jsonl-history}@comma{} the reader both backends share; what Codex records as the user's but wrote itself@comma{} such as the project's @samp{AGENTS.md} and its environment context@comma{} is left out.

The page shows one conversation@comma{} and nothing around it changes its size. The screen supplies only what the transcript cannot@comma{} read by the backend's @code{:screen-state} slot. A status line of one fixed height sits between the conversation and the reply box@comma{} holding the running spinner as plain text@comma{} such as @samp{Forging… 11m 33s · 2.9k tokens}@comma{} or else the mode@comma{} and is cut off rather than wrapped. Text still being written ends the conversation as one more message@comma{} until the transcript holds it. A dialog@comma{} a picker@comma{} a notice that takes input@comma{} or a screen with no input box that is not recognized appears as a card lying over the bottom of the conversation@comma{} marked Waiting on you@comma{} with its text scrollable. Where the card starts errs towards more: at the topmost rule below the nearest message or ended turn@comma{} since a dialog opens with a rule and may hold more@comma{} and with no such mark on the screen the whole screen. Followed to its end@comma{} the conversation shows its last lines above the card@comma{} and it scrolls freely while the card is open. The card's lines are the terminal's@comma{} and are not redacted@comma{} and neither are the status line's.

The transcript is found from the session buffer's own session id@comma{} directory and account@comma{} never from anything in the request@comma{} which can name only a live session and byte offsets. Text passes through @code{agent-log-redact-text} first when the @samp{agent-log} package is installed and @code{agent-log-redact-enabled} is non-nil@comma{} and is hidden if redaction fails. Without it the page shows the text with a warning that secrets are not redacted. Asking for history loads @samp{agent-log-redact}@comma{} which also turns on its redaction of rendered logs. Redaction applies to the transcript only: the status line@comma{} the card@comma{} a message still being written and the whole screen show what the terminal shows. Sessions without a transcript@comma{} which includes Codex sessions and a Claude session before its first prompt@comma{} show the whole screen as the only view@comma{} its lines wrapped to the phone's width. A Claude session that has lost its status link@comma{} so that Emacs can never learn its session id@comma{} shows the whole screen under a note saying its conversation cannot be found@comma{} rather than an empty conversation that never fills. A Codex app-server buffer has its own scrollback.

The session view's Restart button restarts the session under another account@comma{} for a session that has hit its usage limit while you are away. It lists the backend's pools and accounts with each account's latest weekly and session usage@comma{} marking accounts at their limit and disabling logged-out ones. Choosing one calls @code{agent-restart-with-account}@comma{} which does what selecting the account with @code{agent-account-select} and then choosing it at the @code{agent-restart} prompt does: the choice becomes the selection for new sessions@comma{} a pool is routed to one of its members@comma{} and the session resumes its conversation under the new account. It never prompts in Emacs; it refuses a session with captured prompts@comma{} which the account change would orphan.

The New button@comma{} on the session list and next to Restart@comma{} starts a session in any folder of the machine Emacs runs on. It opens a folder browser@comma{} served by @samp{/api/directories}@comma{} that starts in the session's directory@comma{} or@comma{} when opened from the list@comma{} in @code{agent-remote-new-session-directory}@comma{} by default the home directory. Tapping a folder enters it@comma{} Up and Home move back@comma{} and Show hidden adds folders whose names start with a dot. The sort button switches between folders by name and folders most recently changed first@comma{} and the phone remembers the choice. A button for each backend starts a session in the folder on show@comma{} through @code{agent-start-session-in}. When a backend has several accounts or pools@comma{} a picker above its button chooses the one the session runs under@comma{} from the list @code{agent-account-select} offers; a pool is routed to one of its members. Default account leaves the choice to Emacs@comma{} as for a session started in that folder from Emacs@comma{} so @code{agent-account-directory-rules} still apply. The phone remembers each backend's pick. Unlike restarting under another account@comma{} the pick does not change the selection for new sessions started from Emacs. It is @code{agent-start-new-session} without the prompts: the account is the one @code{agent-account-resolve} picks for that directory without asking@comma{} and a second session in the directory gets the first free instance name of the form @samp{new-N}. The page then follows the new session. @code{agent-start-session-beside} is the same start for the directory of a given session@comma{} for use from Lisp. The Exit button offers @code{agent-exit} and @code{agent-exit-without-skills}; both call @code{agent-exit-buffer}@comma{} which never prompts in Emacs either. It refuses a session with captured prompts and does not ask about the session's branches when its process exits@comma{} since the phone has already confirmed the exit. Once Emacs accepts the exit@comma{} the page returns to the list: with the before-exit skills the session stays open@comma{} snoozed@comma{} until they finish@comma{} and the page does not wait for that.

The server listens on @code{agent-remote-listen-address} and @code{agent-remote-port} (default 8787). The listen address defaults to every IPv4 interface@comma{} because the macOS Tailscale app does not deliver connections to a socket bound to the Tailscale address alone. Instead@comma{} the server closes without a response every connection whose peer is outside @code{agent-remote-allowed-networks}@comma{} which defaults to @samp{100.64.0.0/10}@comma{} the range Tailscale assigns to tailnet devices; devices on the local network cannot use it. @code{agent-remote-host} is the address the phone uses. When nil@comma{} it is this machine's Tailscale IPv4 address@comma{} obtained from @code{agent-remote-tailscale-program} when the first request arrives@comma{} so the mode can be enabled while Tailscale is disconnected; until the address is known@comma{} every request is refused as misdirected. Two checks keep web pages in a browser on the same machine from using the server: every request must name @code{agent-remote-host} and the port in its @samp{Host} header@comma{} which defeats DNS rebinding@comma{} and every request that sends input must carry an @samp{X-Agent-Remote} header@comma{} which a cross-origin page cannot add without a preflight the server never approves. There is no further authentication@comma{} so do not add networks that untrusted devices can connect from. A phone that hangs up before its response is written@comma{} because the page was closed or the network dropped mid-request@comma{} gets no response and raises no error in Emacs.

On macOS@comma{} a locked screen hides every Emacs window@comma{} and about a minute later App Nap drops Emacs to background priority. Requests then take seconds@comma{} and the page reports the computer offline until the screen is unlocked. Enabling the mode therefore warns when App Nap is still on for the application running Emacs. @code{agent-remote-disable-app-nap} turns it off by writing that application's @samp{NSAppSleepDisabled} default@comma{} which Emacs reads at launch@comma{} so it takes effect once Emacs is restarted.

The page is @samp{etc/agent-remote.html}@comma{} served from the source checkout. Every response carries an @samp{X-Agent-Remote-Version} header@comma{} a stamp of the loaded @samp{agent-remote.el} and of the page file@comma{} and the page is served with the stamp it was built from. A phone that kept the page open across an update would read the newer API with the older page@comma{} so on a mismatch the page reloads itself@comma{} once per version@comma{} unless a reply is being written or a sheet is open; it then waits for a later response. The + button beside the text box attaches images@comma{} and an image pasted into the text box is attached too where the phone's keyboard and browser allow it. The page shrinks each image to at most 2048 pixels a side@comma{} sends it to @samp{/api/attach}@comma{} which saves it in a temporary file only this user can read@comma{} and shows a thumbnail with a button to remove it. Send adds the files' paths to the reply@comma{} which is how a session gets the image. The system's temporary-file cleanup removes the files. In the Android app@comma{} the + button needs version 1.3 or later.

The @samp{android/} directory holds a single-purpose Android app@comma{} ``Agents''@comma{} for phones without a browser. On first launch it asks for the server address@comma{} the computer's Tailscale address and port@comma{} so one build serves any computer; Change address on its connection-error screen asks again. It accepts plain http only to a Tailscale address@comma{} which the tailnet encrypts@comma{} and shows only that page. A tapped http or https link to another origin opens in the browser@comma{} and any other navigation away from the page is cancelled. @samp{android/build.sh install} builds it with the Android command-line tools@comma{} without Gradle@comma{} and installs it over @samp{adb}; @samp{AGENT_REMOTE_URL}@comma{} when set@comma{} is pre-filled as the address. The newest release is always at @uref{https://github.com/benthamite/agent/releases/download/android-latest/agent-remote.apk}. @samp{android/release.sh VERSION CHANGES-FILE} publishes a new one: it bumps @samp{android/version.env}@comma{} builds the app@comma{} refuses one signed with a different key from the previous release@comma{} publishes it as @samp{android-vVERSION}@comma{} and moves that link to it; @samp{--dry-run} shows the release notes without changing anything.

