@code{agent-report}@comma{} @samp{t b} in @code{agent-menu}@comma{} files a bug report or a feature idea as a GitHub issue on @code{agent-report-repository}@comma{} @samp{benthamite/agent} by default@comma{} for the maintainer to act on. It opens a buffer for the description; @samp{C-c C-c} (@code{agent-report-send}) files it and @samp{C-c C-k} (@code{agent-report-cancel}) discards it. The first line becomes the issue's title@comma{} and the body adds where the report came from@comma{} the package's commit@comma{} the Emacs version and the system. Filing runs @code{agent-report-gh-program}@comma{} the GitHub CLI@comma{} as @samp{gh issue create}@comma{} without blocking Emacs; the buffer is killed once the issue exists and its URL is shown@comma{} and kept when filing fails@comma{} with the reason. The CLI must be installed and logged in (@samp{gh auth login})@comma{} and the failure message says so. The phone page's Report button@comma{} at the right of the session list's bar@comma{} does the same through @samp{/api/report}@comma{} which answers once the CLI has finished: the server's handlers may defer their response this way when they would otherwise wait. The page shows a link to the new issue@comma{} or why it was not filed@comma{} and keeps the text until it is.

@node Branch navigation
@section Branch navigation

@findex agent-resume
@findex agent-create-branch
@findex agent-switch-branch
A branch is a session forked from another one: it starts with the parent's whole conversation and then diverges. Branching lets you try a different approach without losing the state you built up@comma{} and the three commands here work the same way whichever backend the session belongs to.

@code{agent-create-branch}@comma{} bound to @samp{b}@comma{} forks the session in the current buffer and opens the fork in its own buffer under an instance name derived from the time of the fork. By default the branch shares the parent's working tree@comma{} which is what starting a second session in the same project would give you. With a prefix argument it also creates a git worktree on a fresh branch under @code{agent-branch-worktree-directory} and runs the fork there@comma{} so that destructive git operations in one branch cannot disturb the other. The worktree starts at the parent's HEAD@comma{} so uncommitted parent changes are not carried into it; use the isolated form when concurrent git work across branches is a real concern@comma{} and the default otherwise. Before the fork starts@comma{} the backend's @code{:prepare-fork} slot makes the parent session visible from the new directory@comma{} which is what a backend that stores its transcripts per project directory needs in order to resume a session recorded elsewhere.

A branch is an ordinary session: select an open one with @code{agent-select-session} and resume a closed one with @code{agent-resume}. The obsolete @code{agent-switch-branch}@comma{} no longer in the menu@comma{} shows every session sharing a fork ancestor with the current one as an indented tree@comma{} each entry carrying its first prompt and its timestamp and the session you are in marked with an asterisk@comma{} and switches to the selected session: to its live buffer when it still has one@comma{} and otherwise by resuming it in a new buffer. It reports that there is nothing to navigate when the current session has no relatives.

@code{agent-resume}@comma{} bound to @samp{o}@comma{} resumes a past session of the current backend@comma{} or of a backend it asks for when no session buffer names one@comma{} and passes its prefix argument through to the backend@comma{} where it means the most recent session. A Claude session whose terminal closed while it was working keeps running in the background@comma{} and the CLI refuses to resume it; every non-fork Claude resume therefore checks @samp{claude agents --json} first and@comma{} when the session is still running in the background@comma{} opens the buffer with @samp{claude attach} instead. An attached session receives no initial prompt and none of @code{claude-code-program-switches}@comma{} which the running session already has and which would otherwise precede the subcommand.

Both backends supply the lineage these commands read@comma{} through the @code{:session-headers} slot@comma{} and each derives it from what its CLI already records. Claude Code writes one transcript per session inside a per-project directory@comma{} so the scan reads the first line of each transcript in the project the current session belongs to. That line is read whole however long it is@comma{} since a session started with a queued prompt records the entire prompt there. Codex writes one rollout file per session@comma{} all under a single sessions directory@comma{} so the scan reads the @code{session_meta} record opening each rollout@comma{} keeps the ones whose recorded working directory matches@comma{} and takes the parent from @code{forked_from_id}. Because that directory holds every Codex session on the machine@comma{} the scan is bounded by a start timestamp when the caller only needs one session's descendants.

Codex records subagent threads with the same @code{forked_from_id} field@comma{} and those are deliberately left out of the tree. They are forks as far as the file format is concerned@comma{} but they are not branches of your conversation: they are threads the model spawned to do work on its own@comma{} and offering to switch into one would be offering something a user never chose to create.

@node Org TODO commands
@section Org TODO commands

@findex agent-send-todo-at-point
The @samp{agent-todo} module turns an Org TODO entry into work for an AI session@comma{} with either backend.

@code{agent-send-todo-at-point} formats the heading and body of the TODO at point@comma{} or of the TODO the agenda line at point shows@comma{} as a prompt and submits it to a session started for it; it is not a menu entry of its own@comma{} since the menu reaches the same routing through @code{agent-act-on-thing-at-point}. With a prefix argument it submits the prompt to a running session chosen by completion. Like a Slack message@comma{} a TODO names no directory@comma{} so the project is read from the account's sources and ranked by the TODO's own text (@ref{Project sources, , Project sources}). It no longer looks for the session belonging to the current file's project: an Org file of tasks rarely sits in the repository those tasks are about. When @code{agent-todo-in-progress-keyword} is set@comma{} the heading moves to that keyword once the prompt has been delivered. The heading is remembered as a marker@comma{} so the move lands on the heading the prompt came from however far point has travelled while the project was being chosen@comma{} and an agenda the command was called from is rebuilt so that its line shows the new keyword. A heading deleted in the meantime is left alone: the state change happens after the session exists but before it is shown@comma{} so an error there would leave a running session unshown.

@node Chief-of-staff commands
@section Chief-of-staff commands

@findex agent-chief-start
@findex agent-chief-start-session
