The Codex commands expose workflows that are specific to the Codex CLI; handoff@comma{} restart@comma{} exit@comma{} skill execution@comma{} backtrace debugging@comma{} and Slack-message routing use the unified @code{agent-*} commands described above. @code{agent-codex-start-or-switch} starts Codex under the selected account when no Codex sessions exist@comma{} otherwise it opens the shared switcher. @code{agent-codex-select-account} persists an account choice by name and applies it to new interactive sessions and non-interactive @samp{codex exec} runs. Skills run non-interactively with @samp{codex exec}@comma{} using local skill metadata when available and falling back to a slash invocation otherwise; additional programmatic skill directories are configured through @code{agent-codex-programmatic-skill-directories}.

When @code{agent-restart} operates on a Codex session@comma{} it resumes codex.el's canonical session identity under the chosen account and current default @code{codex-terminal-backend}; app-server sessions use the native app-server resume path and terminal backends use @samp{codex resume}. Changing @code{codex-terminal-backend} and then restarting a Codex session switches the relaunched session to that backend. The unified commands cover both Codex resuming and Codex forking: @code{agent-resume} resumes a past session of whichever backend the context names@comma{} and @code{agent-create-branch} forks the current session of either backend@comma{} reaching Codex through @samp{codex fork} on the terminal backends and @samp{thread/fork} on the app-server backend. @code{agent-codex-resume} and @code{agent-codex-fork} still expose the CLI's own subcommands and remain available through @samp{M-x}@comma{} though neither is in @code{agent-menu} any more. @code{agent-codex-fork} is not redundant: it forks a session you pick from Codex's own history@comma{} whereas @code{agent-create-branch} forks the session you are sitting in.

