The function @code{agent-register-backend} registers an AI tool by constructing an @code{agent-backend} struct from keyword arguments. Backend modules call it at load time. Keywords are validated against the struct's slot names@comma{} and the required slots @code{:buffer-p}@comma{} @code{:find-all-buffers}@comma{} and @code{:start-session} are checked so the shared session layer can rely on a consistent interface.

The full slot set is: identity and presentation (@code{name}@comma{} @code{label}@comma{} @code{icon}@comma{} @code{program})@comma{} buffer discovery (@code{buffer-p}@comma{} @code{find-all-buffers}@comma{} @code{find-buffers-for-dir})@comma{} session lifecycle (@code{start-session}@comma{} @code{session-identity}@comma{} @code{restart-options})@comma{} send primitives (@code{send-string}@comma{} @code{send-return}@comma{} @code{submit})@comma{} session state probes (@code{waiting-p}@comma{} @code{busy-p}@comma{} @code{background-tasks-p}@comma{} @code{status-unknown-p}@comma{} @code{display-name-suffix}@comma{} @code{notify})@comma{} accounts (@code{account-env-var}@comma{} @code{accounts}@comma{} @code{account-file}@comma{} @code{shared-config-items}@comma{} @code{canonical-home}@comma{} @code{account-init}@comma{} @code{credential-file}@comma{} @code{login-args}@comma{} @code{usage-fetch})@comma{} skills (@code{run-prompt}@comma{} @code{skill-roots}@comma{} @code{skill-command-prefix})@comma{} session history (@code{session-headers}@comma{} @code{session-prompt}@comma{} @code{resume}@comma{} @code{prepare-fork}@comma{} @code{session-history}@comma{} @code{screen-state})@comma{} integration hooks (@code{sync-theme})@comma{} and exit safety (@code{before-exit-ready-to-close-p}@comma{} @code{before-kill-check}@comma{} @code{idle-p}@comma{} @code{reported-idle-p}).

The @code{:start-session} slot starts a new session parameterized by an @code{agent-session} identity struct@comma{} optionally with an initial prompt or a resume id. The @code{:session-identity} slot returns a live session buffer's CLI session id@comma{} used by @code{agent-restart} to resume in place. @code{agent-start-session} is the core dispatcher that starts a session through its backend's @code{:start-session} function@comma{} passing any extra backend-specific options through. It is also the entry point for starting sessions from Lisp: unlike @code{agent-start-new-session}@comma{} it never prompts for a backend or an account.

@vindex agent-inhibit-prompts
A prompt in the minibuffer blocks Emacs@comma{} which freezes a caller such as @samp{emacsclient --eval} that nobody is watching. Bind @code{agent-inhibit-prompts} to non-nil around such a call and the session start paths stop asking. A question with a default takes it: an unset account selection falls to the backend's default account and is not persisted. A question with none signals a @code{user-error} instead: the choice among several registered backends@comma{} since no backend is the default@comma{} and the instance name of a second session in a directory@comma{} since @code{agent-start-session} needs one to tell the two apart. With the variable nil@comma{} as in interactive use@comma{} every prompt stays as it was.

The @code{:send-string} slot inserts text into a session's prompt without submitting it@comma{} @code{:send-return} submits the pending prompt@comma{} and @code{:submit} atomically inserts and submits; the core wrappers @code{agent-send-string}@comma{} @code{agent-send-return}@comma{} and @code{agent-submit} dispatch through these slots and emit the corresponding session events.

The slots that carry the unified session commands are all optional@comma{} and a backend that omits one simply does not support the commands dispatching on it. @code{:resume} resumes a past session@comma{} receiving the command's prefix argument@comma{} and backs @code{agent-resume}. @code{:session-headers} returns a hash table mapping session id to a plist of @code{:session-id}@comma{} @code{:forked-from} and @code{:timestamp}@comma{} called with a session buffer and optionally with the one session whose descendants the caller cares about@comma{} so a backend that keeps every session in one directory can bound its scan; @code{:session-prompt} enriches one such header with the session's @code{:first-prompt}. Together they back the branch check before a kill and the obsolete @code{agent-switch-branch}. @code{:prepare-fork} makes a session recorded under one directory visible from another@comma{} which is what lets an isolated branch run in a fresh worktree. @code{:session-history} reads a bounded stretch of a session's past conversation for the phone page: it is called with a session buffer@comma{} a byte limit@comma{} and optional byte offsets to read before and after@comma{} and returns nil when the session has no transcript yet. @code{:screen-state} reads what a session's screen shows that the transcript cannot@comma{} for the phone page: called with the screen's text@comma{} it returns a plist of @code{:status}@comma{} @code{:mode}@comma{} @code{:running}@comma{} @code{:writing}@comma{} @code{:card}@comma{} @code{:options} and @code{:dialog}@comma{} the last non-nil when the card has replaced the input box@comma{} so that typed text would go to it. Only Claude supplies these two. @code{:icon-svg} is the SVG source of the backend's icon@comma{} which the phone page shows on each session's card.

@code{agent-display-name} returns a human-readable label for a session buffer. It starts with the project name@comma{} adds an instance name when needed to disambiguate duplicates@comma{} and lets backends append their own suffixes. The Claude backend uses this to expose branch identity in the modeline and switcher.

@code{agent-parse-skill-frontmatter} reads @samp{SKILL.md} frontmatter shared by Claude and Codex skill runners. It recognizes names@comma{} descriptions@comma{} argument hints@comma{} argument sources@comma{} argument choices@comma{} defaults@comma{} multiplicity@comma{} user-invocable flags@comma{} and model overrides.

@code{agent-svg-icon} renders SVG strings as inline images@comma{} using the current face foreground when the SVG uses @samp{currentColor}. Backends use this for modeline and transient display and can fall back to text abbreviations when SVG rendering is unavailable.

@node Modeline and status helpers
@section Modeline and status helpers

@findex agent-doom-modeline-session-status
@samp{doom-modeline} is optional. When it is loaded@comma{} whether before or after @samp{agent}@comma{} @samp{agent-doom-modeline.el} defines the @samp{ai-session} modeline: the backend icon@comma{} the session name@comma{} the model@comma{} the reasoning effort@comma{} the account@comma{} and the status the session's agent last published@comma{} each separated by a bar. A field with no value is left out. On the right it shows doom-modeline's miscellaneous information and the time@comma{} but not the major mode or the process state@comma{} which would read the same in every session buffer. @code{agent-doom-modeline-session-status} returns that text for the current buffer@comma{} for use in another modeline. Without @samp{doom-modeline}@comma{} the file is never loaded and session buffers keep the standard mode line.

@findex agent-claude-status-model
