@findex agent-claude-status-weekly-usage
@findex agent-codex-status-model
@findex agent-codex-status-effort
@findex agent-codex-status-duration-ms
The Claude and Codex status helpers are intended for modeline integrations such as @samp{doom-modeline}. Session buffers use doom-modeline's @samp{ai-session} modeline when one is defined. Turning @code{doom-modeline-mode} on sets the @samp{main} modeline in every buffer@comma{} and once the mode has been toggled interactively@comma{} every theme load turns it on again; both backends therefore set @samp{ai-session} again in each session buffer whenever the mode is turned on. Claude status functions read the current session status JSON and expose model@comma{} reasoning effort@comma{} cost@comma{} context@comma{} duration@comma{} and token information. Claude usage functions expose account-level utilization returned by the Anthropic usage endpoint. Codex status functions expose the current session model@comma{} reasoning effort@comma{} and elapsed session duration; the model helper falls back to the configured model when the session has no live model value. When Codex has no explicit reasoning-effort override@comma{} the status helper reports Codex's default @samp{medium} effort. The session switcher uses backend busy@comma{} input-readiness@comma{} and background-work detectors to avoid showing actively responding sessions as waiting@comma{} and to distinguish truly idle waiting sessions from sessions that are accepting input while background terminals@comma{} agents@comma{} or tasks are still running. For Claude sessions the display comes from Claude Code's own report of each session@comma{} read with @samp{claude agents --json}@comma{} the supported way to read session state from outside a session. It lists the live interactive sessions of one config directory with a status of busy@comma{} waiting or idle@comma{} and busy includes background work left running after a turn ends. Idle or waiting shows the session waiting (@code{:waiting-p})@comma{} and busy once the turn has ended shows background work (@code{:background-tasks-p}); hooks still mark when a turn starts and ends. A query (@code{agent-claude-agents-command}) runs in the background@comma{} once per config directory. Its result is stored from a timer once the process exits@comma{} never inside the process sentinel@comma{} and a further query for the same directory waits until that result is stored. A query whose process cannot start counts as a failed query. Claude Code takes a moment to register a change: a query started the instant a prompt is sent still reads idle. So a session event@comma{} which marks a turn boundary such as a prompt sent from Emacs or a stop (@code{agent-session-event-functions} runs for every session event@comma{} including one that changes no state)@comma{} makes every earlier result stale for that session and schedules a query @code{agent-claude-status-settle-time} seconds later. A result counts as current for a session only when its query began at least that long after the session's latest session event; until then the session-event state machine decides. A prompt sent from Emacs has to be handled as a session event because no hook reaches Emacs for it: the state hook drops activity events while the state file already reads busy. Other hook traffic@comma{} such as a subagent's tool calls@comma{} marks no turn boundary: it refreshes the result@comma{} no more often than @code{agent-claude-agents-status-min-interval} seconds@comma{} without making it stale@comma{} since a teammate working under an idle lead would otherwise keep every result stale for as long as it worked. A display also queries again when the result is older than @code{agent-claude-agents-status-max-age} seconds. A session is shown as unknown (@code{:status-unknown-p})@comma{} never guessed@comma{} while no result exists for its config directory@comma{} when the latest query failed (which also raises a warning)@comma{} or when a current result does not list it. After each query@comma{} every session whose display differs from what the hooks alone would show is logged to @samp{*Messages*}@comma{} without appearing in the echo area@comma{} once per change@comma{} so the two sources stay comparable. What the hooks alone show@comma{} which decided the display until October 2026 and is kept for that comparison@comma{} is built from Claude Code's own list of the session's background tasks@comma{} which its @samp{Stop} and @samp{SubagentStop} hook payloads carry as @samp{background_tasks}@comma{} with each shell@comma{} Monitor@comma{} background agent@comma{} and teammate and its status@comma{} including those started by subagents. The bundled @samp{record-background-tasks.sh} hook@comma{} installed by @code{agent-claude-ensure-background-hook-config} (part of @code{agent-claude-setup-config})@comma{} writes each payload to a file in @code{agent-claude-status-directory} keyed like the statusline file@comma{} and @code{agent-claude--background-tasks} counts the tasks it reports as running. A task that ends while the session waits wakes Claude with a task notification@comma{} so the turn that follows ends with a fresh list; a task killed without such a turn keeps counting until the next one. A payload without the field raises a warning instead of passing as no tasks. Agent-team teammates are the exception: the list reports a teammate as running until it is shut down@comma{} including while it sits idle waiting for a message. The same hook@comma{} also installed on @samp{SubagentStart} and @samp{TeammateIdle}@comma{} therefore keeps one file per working subagent or teammate in a @samp{.agents} directory keyed like the statusline file: @samp{SubagentStart}@comma{} which fires each time a teammate takes a message@comma{} creates it@comma{} and @samp{SubagentStop} or @samp{TeammateIdle} removes it. Teammate tasks count only while that directory has an entry. The list does not say which teammate a task is@comma{} so while any one works@comma{} every teammate task counts. A Monitor that Claude Code arms by itself when the session publishes an artifact@comma{} to watch it for comments (described as @samp{live updates for artifact … (auto-armed on publish)} and carrying no command)@comma{} does not count either: it runs until the session ends@comma{} so it would show every finished session that published an artifact as busy. A @samp{claude -p} run from a session's shell inherits the session's environment@comma{} so its hooks would report as the session; its @samp{Stop} would mark the session waiting in the middle of a turn. @code{agent-claude--record-session-id} therefore writes the session id the status line reports to a @samp{.sid} file keyed like the statusline file@comma{} and the bundled hook scripts (through @samp{session-owner.sh}) and @code{agent-claude--foreign-hook-p} drop a payload naming another session. A payload without a session id@comma{} or one arriving before the status line has reported the id@comma{} is accepted. The Remote Control task UI also counts. The footer's task counts are not used@comma{} because its @samp{← N agents} indicator counts agents across every session on the machine. A session counts as waiting only when it is blocked on the user@comma{} meaning it has stopped and will not proceed until the user submits something. Being able to queue text mid-turn is not waiting@comma{} since both CLIs accept typed input at any point. Codex app-server sessions learn turn boundaries from the JSON-RPC stream@comma{} so an inactive turn on a live app server is authoritative for waiting and an active turn is authoritative for busy@comma{} regardless of whether the prompt accepts input. Terminal sessions have no such signal and fall back to the session-event state machine and to scanning rendered status text. For Claude@comma{} @code{agent-claude--terminal-waiting-p} reports idle when the rendered tail shows the input prompt and no running-turn spinner line (a glyph@comma{} a verb ending in an ellipsis@comma{} and a parenthesised elapsed time@comma{} e.g. @samp{✢ Billowing… (4m 35s)}; the older @samp{esc to interrupt} hint and the background-task @samp{esc to give additional instructions} prompt also count as running). It is registered as the backend's @code{:idle-p} — not @code{:before-exit-ready-to-close-p}@comma{} whose veto runs on every chain step@comma{} where the screen's redraw lag behind the Stop hook would strand the chain — and consulted only by the before-exit chain and the escape interrupt check described in @ref{Session state machine, , Session state machine}: when arming@comma{} a busy session whose terminal affirms idleness arms immediately@comma{} and the watchdog advances a chain stranded in @code{waiting-for-idle} whose stop event never arrived — a turn the CLI accepts and then drops@comma{} for instance at the context limit@comma{} fires no Stop hook. Screen scraping is not reliable enough to drive the switcher on its own@comma{} so display state stays with the session-event state machine; the interrupt check consults the screen only right after the user sends an escape. A @code{busy} state younger than @code{agent-claude-terminal-idle-grace} seconds is trusted over the screen@comma{} since the spinner takes a moment to appear after a submission. Codex prompt submission clears the previous waiting marker at the target buffer@comma{} so a session does not remain colored as waiting after the user sends a new prompt.

@node Transient menu
@chapter Transient menu

@findex agent-menu
@code{agent-menu} is a single static prefix defined in @samp{agent.el}@comma{} laid out in four columns: Sessions with Account beneath it@comma{} Current session@comma{} Tools with Prompts beneath it@comma{} and Options. Nothing in the layout depends on which backends are loaded@comma{} because every command in it is backend-agnostic. Each one resolves a backend from the current session buffer@comma{} or asks when nothing names one@comma{} and then dispatches through that backend's slots@comma{} so the same key operates on a Claude session and a Codex session alike. There is no longer any mechanism for a backend module to add entries to the menu@comma{} and this is deliberate: a backend contributes an implementation for a slot@comma{} and the command that calls that slot lives in @samp{agent.el}.

In the Options group@comma{} @samp{-c} selects an account. It acts on the current or prompted backend@comma{} while its value shows every registered backend's current account at once@comma{} so one entry replaces the per-backend account selectors the menu used to carry. The infix shares its implementation with the per-backend account commands in @samp{agent-account.el}@comma{} and syncs the selected account's config home as soon as the selection is made.

The keys @samp{a}@comma{} @samp{b}@comma{} @samp{g}@comma{} @samp{m}@comma{} @samp{p}@comma{} @samp{t} and @samp{-} are prefixes of the two-key entries@comma{} so to extend the menu with @code{transient-append-suffix} or the like@comma{} do not bind any of them on its own. A key that is a prefix of another makes the whole menu fail to open@comma{} with @samp{Wrong type argument: command@comma{} (keymap ...)}@comma{} and @code{transient-get-suffix} cannot warn of it@comma{} since it reports such a key as free.

Boolean menu infixes use a shared transient class that toggles the bound variable each time it is invoked. The theme-sync infix additionally calls @code{agent-sync-theme-now} when enabling theme sync@comma{} so backend configuration files are updated immediately.

